window.PAGES_TR = window.PAGES_TR || { g6: [], g7: [] };
(function (P) {
P[0] = [`<h2>What is chemistry?</h2>
<h3>Think about it!</h3>
<div class="dlg"><p>In the Rishtan pottery workshop there is always movement and creativity. As the master potter spins the clay, delicate items take shape from a simple lump of clay.</p><p>At first the clay is soft and shapeless, but with the potter's knowledge and experience it turns into a beautiful bowl, dish or jug.</p><p><b>Chemistry is just like that — it studies substances and reveals the secrets of how they change!</b></p></div>
<ol><li>What other substances besides clay do we work with?</li><li>What does studying chemistry give us?</li><li>In your opinion, what changes take place when a pot is made?</li></ol>
<h3>Key concepts</h3>
<table><tr><th>Chemistry</th><th>Substance</th><th>Object</th></tr><tr><td>The science that studies substances and how they change.</td><td>A type of matter that has mass and makes up objects.</td><td>Any item or thing around us that we can see and touch.</td></tr></table>
<div class="box">Chemistry helps us understand processes in nature: cooking, baking bread, iron rusting, water evaporating and many other phenomena are connected with chemistry.</div>`,
`<h3>Small experiment: Vinegar and soda — a magical meeting</h3>
<p><b>You will need:</b> vinegar (CH₃COOH), baking soda, a small glass, a glass jar or bottle.</p>
<p><b>Steps:</b></p><ol><li>Put 1 spoonful of soda into the glass.</li><li>Pour a little vinegar on top.</li><li>Watch what happens.</li></ol>
<p><b>Observe and explain:</b> Bubbles appeared. A weightless-looking gas (carbon dioxide) formed. <b>This is a chemical change!</b></p>
<h3>Chemistry detective</h3><p>Decide whether each example is a substance or an object and write your answer in the table: gold, glass jar, air, water, wooden table…</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Note:</b> a substance may be invisible (for example, air), but it still has mass.</div>
<h3>STEM corner: Chemistry in pottery!</h3>
<p>Clay contains elements such as silicon (Si), aluminium (Al), iron (Fe) and calcium (Ca).</p>
<p>Clay (raw material) → high temperature (kiln) → chemical change → strong pot (a new object). When fired at <b>900–1100 °C</b>, chemical changes take place.</p>
<p><b>Question:</b> What happens if the kiln temperature is not high enough?</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Behind every object there is work, knowledge and patience. The master potter creates each pot with love. Let us do all our work responsibly too. Both knowledge and craft make a person great!</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 5–6 sentences on the topic "Chemistry in my life".</li><li>Write 5 objects and 5 substances found at home and make a table.</li><li>Repeat the vinegar and soda experiment, draw the result in your notebook and explain it.</li></ol>
<div class="box g"><b>What did we learn today?</b> What is chemistry? What is a substance? What is an object? What is a chemical change?</div>`];

P[1] = [`<h2>Laboratory equipment and safety</h2>
<h3>Key concepts</h3>
<ul><li><b>LABORATORY</b> — a special room where scientific experiments are carried out.</li><li><b>REAGENT</b> — a substance that takes part in a chemical reaction.</li><li><b>SAFETY RULES</b> — rules followed in order to work safely.</li></ul>
<div class="dlg"><p>Today you have come to a new, exciting and mysterious place — the chemistry laboratory!</p><p>Teacher Kamol told you: "Every great discovery begins with a simple experiment."</p><p>He added a few drops of a blue reagent to clear water in a test tube. In an instant the colour of the water changed! "Why did that happen?" you wondered.</p><p>Every piece of equipment and every substance in a chemistry lab opens a door to new knowledge! But remember: knowledge is safe and useful only when it goes together with care.</p></div>
<h3>Think about it</h3><ol><li>Why must we follow the rules in a laboratory?</li><li>What dangers could arise if we are not careful?</li><li>How does laboratory equipment help you?</li></ol>
<h3>Did you know?</h3><ul><li>According to the textbook, the world's first chemistry laboratory was set up in Bukhara in the 9th century by Abulqosim az-Zahraviy.</li><li>Today laboratories exist in hospitals, schools, factories and research centres.</li><li>A clean and tidy laboratory is the key to safety and success.</li></ul>`,
`<h3>Yes or no?</h3><p>Are these statements true? (Yes — ✓, No — ✗)</p>
<ol><li>You may eat in the laboratory.</li><li>Reagents may be unlabelled.</li><li>Goggles and a lab coat must be worn.</li><li>A test tube should be held pointing at someone else.</li><li>If water is spilled, you must tell the teacher immediately.</li><li>After using equipment, there is no need to put it back.</li><li>Hands must be washed before and after an experiment.</li><li>Flammable substances may be used near a flame.</li></ol>
<h3>STEM corner: Water filtration experiment</h3><p><b>Equipment:</b> a glass, muddy water, filter paper, a funnel.</p><p>Pour muddy water into a glass → place the funnel on a clean glass and put the filter paper in it → slowly pour in the muddy water → the water is cleaned! Why?</p><p><b>Question:</b> What job did the filter paper do? Write your answer in your notebook.</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Carefulness is the best friend of everyone who has knowledge. A person who follows the rules protects themselves, others and nature. Let us be responsible in everything we do!</p>
<h3>Chemistry detective</h3><p>The pictures show laboratory equipment and materials. Write their names and explain what they are used for.</p>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>Laboratory tests are very important for diagnosing diseases:</p><ul><li>Blood test — detects anaemia.</li><li>Urine test — shows kidney diseases.</li><li>Microscope examination — detects bacteria and viruses.</li></ul><p>So the laboratory is the guardian of our health!</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 5 rules on the topic "Safety rules in the laboratory".</li><li>Find 3 items at home that are connected with chemistry and explain.</li><li>Draw laboratory equipment and write their names (at least 5).</li></ol>`];

P[2] = [`<h2>Substances and their properties</h2>
<div class="box y"><b>Kitchen rules:</b> wash your hands before entering; be careful; we don't touch without permission; we stay neat and tidy.</div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3><ul><li><b>PROPERTIES OF A SUBSTANCE</b> — the special features and characteristics that distinguish it from other substances.</li><li><b>PHYSICAL PROPERTIES</b> — features that can be observed or measured without changing what the substance is made of.</li><li><b>CHEMICAL PROPERTIES</b> — the ability of a substance to react with another substance and form a new substance.</li></ul>
<div class="dlg"><p>Today in the school canteen the cook showed us different substances: salt, sugar, vinegar, bread, milk, vegetable oil…</p><p>Teacher Kamol said: "Every substance has its own properties. If we know them, we will use them correctly and stay safe."</p><p>So we decided: from today we are <b>Chemistry Detectives!</b> We will uncover the secret of every substance!</p></div>
<h3>Compare</h3><p>Look at these substances and compare their properties (colour, smell, does it dissolve in water?): sugar, vinegar, vegetable oil.</p>
<ul><li>The properties of a substance depend on its components and structure.</li><li>One kind of substance always has its own particular properties.</li><li>Knowing properties helps us recognise a substance, use it correctly and stay safe.</li><li>In chemistry, substances are studied through experiment, observation and measurement.</li></ul>`,
`<h3>Small experiment: Dissolving salt in water</h3><p><b>Equipment:</b> 2 clear glasses, water, a spoon, salt.</p><ol><li>Pour plain water into glass 1.</li><li>Put 1 teaspoon of salt into glass 2 and stir.</li><li>Stir until the salt has completely dissolved and observe.</li></ol><p><b>Conclusion:</b> salt dissolves in water but does not itself change. This is a <b>physical property (solubility)</b>.</p>
<h3>STEM corner: Sorting substances by their properties</h3><p>Sort into physical or chemical properties: iron rusting, salt dissolving in water, paper burning, milk turning sour, gold being shiny.</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Every substance can be useful or harmful because of its properties. Knowing substances and using them correctly is very important for our health and our environment.</p>
<h3>Yes or no?</h3><ol><li>When sugar dissolves in water, its composition changes.</li><li>Iron rusting is a chemical property.</li><li>Colour, smell and taste are examples of physical properties.</li><li>Oil dissolves in water.</li><li>The properties of a substance help us recognise it.</li><li>In cooking, acetic acid takes part in reactions.</li></ol>
<h3>Chemistry detective</h3><p>By which properties can these substances be recognised? Tick: colour, smell, taste, state, solubility — citric acid, baking soda, copper (metal).</p>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><ul><li>Medicines are chosen according to their properties.</li><li>Aspirin dissolves in water (physical property).</li><li>In the body aspirin takes part in a chemical reaction and reduces pain (chemical property).</li><li>Every medicine is used in a safe dose.</li></ul>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Find 5 different substances at home and write 3 physical properties of each.</li><li>Explain with an example that iron rusting is a chemical change.</li><li>Copy 3 terms from today's lesson into your notebook and learn their definitions.</li></ol>`];

P[3] = [`<h2>The composition of matter</h2>
<p><b>What is everything we see made of?</b></p>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>Teacher Kamol pointed at an ice cube in a glass of drinking water and asked:</p><p>— Children, what do you think this ice cube is made of?</p><p>Let's go from big to small and uncover the secret of the ice!</p></div>
<h3>Composition of matter: let's zoom in!</h3><p><b>SUBSTANCE → MOLECULE → ATOM → STRUCTURE OF THE ATOM</b><br>Ice cube → water (H₂O) → a water molecule → an oxygen atom (O) → the structure of the oxygen atom.</p>
<h3>Terms</h3><ul><li><b>Molecule</b> — the smallest particle that keeps the properties of a substance.</li><li><b>Atom</b> — an electrically neutral particle made of a positively charged nucleus and negatively charged electrons.</li></ul>
<h3>Compare</h3><table><tr><th>Comparison</th><th>Feature</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Object ≠ substance</td><td>An object has a definite shape and volume; the substance is what the object is made of.</td><td>Ice cube ≠ water</td></tr><tr><td>Substance ≠ molecule</td><td>A substance is made of many molecules.</td><td>Water ≠ H₂O molecules</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule ≠ atom</td><td>A molecule is made of atoms.</td><td>H₂O ≠ H and O atoms</td></tr></table>
<div class="box"><b>Did you know?</b> A cup of water contains about 10²⁴ (or more) water molecules!</div>`,
`<h3>Yes or no?</h3><ol><li>An ice cube is a substance.</li><li>A water molecule can be seen with the eye.</li><li>An atom is the smallest particle of a substance.</li><li>A proton has a positive charge.</li><li>A neutron is an uncharged particle.</li><li>A molecule is made of atoms.</li></ol>
<h3>STEM corner</h3><p>Draw the atomic structure of lithium (Li). Show the electrons in the 1st and 2nd shells.</p><p>Nucleus: protons (p⁺), neutrons (n⁰). Electrons (e⁻).</p><p><b>Tip:</b> a lithium (Li) atom has 3 protons. Don't forget to place the electrons in shells!</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Do not ignore small things. Even invisible tiny particles are very important in our lives. Through knowledge we understand them and uncover the secrets of the world.</p>
<h3>Chemistry detective</h3><p>Which particles are these substances made of? Draw lines to match: oxygen gas (O₂) — molecules; table salt (NaCl) — ions.</p>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>The air we breathe contains oxygen gas (O₂). It enters our body and plays an important role in releasing energy in our cells. Without oxygen there would be no life.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Explain the connection between object, substance, molecule and atom with a drawing.</li><li>Draw the structure of the oxygen atom and show its electrons (shells: 1st shell, 2nd shell).</li><li>Write 5–6 sentences on "The composition of matter".</li></ol>`];

P[4] = [`<h2>The difference between a chemical element and an atom</h2>
<p><b>Key question:</b> Why do all oxygen atoms belong to one element, yet some of them differ from each other?</p>
<p>Models: 8 protons + 8 neutrons; 8 protons + 9 neutrons; 8 protons + 10 neutrons.</p>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>Today Teacher Kamol put models of three oxygen atoms on the table.</p><p>— Children, all three of these are atoms of the element oxygen. But why are their masses not the same?</p><p>Jasur was surprised: — What is the difference inside them?</p><p>— Today we will learn the secrets of the atom, the chemical element and the isotope together.</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3><ul><li><b>ATOM</b> — a particle made of a positively charged nucleus and negatively charged electrons.</li><li><b>CHEMICAL ELEMENT</b> — the set of atoms that have the same number of protons. Example: all atoms with 8 protons are the element oxygen.</li><li><b>ISOTOPE</b> — atoms of the same chemical element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.</li></ul>
<div class="box"><b>Did you know?</b> All atoms in nature belong to one of the 118 chemical elements. An element's atomic number equals its number of protons.</div>
<table><tr><th>Atom</th><th>Chemical element</th></tr><tr><td>One particle made of a nucleus and electrons. E.g. one oxygen atom.</td><td>A set of atoms with the same number of protons. E.g. hydrogen — 1, carbon — 6, oxygen — 8.</td></tr></table>
<p><b>ATOM → CHEMICAL ELEMENT → ISOTOPE:</b> one oxygen atom → the element oxygen (many oxygen atoms) → same number of protons, different numbers of neutrons.</p>`,
`<h3>Yes or no?</h3><ol><li>An atom consists of a positive nucleus and electrons.</li><li>Atoms with the same number of protons belong to one element.</li><li>In isotopes the number of protons is different.</li><li>An oxygen atom has 8 protons.</li><li>A chemical element consists of a single atom.</li><li>In isotopes the number of neutrons can be different.</li></ol>
<h3>STEM corner: Let's make an atomic model</h3><p>Using coloured plasticine or paper, make a model of an oxygen atom (proton, neutron, electron).</p><p><b>Question:</b> Which particle determines which element an atom belongs to?</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Just as every person is unique, atoms also have their own structure. Science teaches us to notice even small differences and draw correct conclusions.</p>
<h3>Chemistry detective</h3><p>Match the atoms with the correct element by their number of protons (e.g. 6 → carbon (C)).</p>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>Doctors use some isotopes to diagnose and treat diseases. For example, radioactive isotopes help study how internal organs work during medical examinations. Isotopes help medicine.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write the difference between an atom and a chemical element.</li><li>Give 3 examples of chemical elements.</li><li>Explain how isotopes differ from each other.</li></ol>
<div class="box g"><b>What did we learn today?</b> What is an atom? What is a chemical element? The difference between an atom and an element. What is an isotope?</div>`];
})(PAGES_TR.g6);
(function (P) {
P[5] = [`<h2>Types of particles: atom, molecule and ion — the difference</h2>
<p><b>Key question:</b> Why are some substances made of single atoms, some of molecules and others of ions? (Pictures: a water molecule, a neon atom.)</p>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>Teacher Kamol put three models on the table.</p><p>— Children, these three look different, but all of them are particles.</p><p>Asal asked: — What is the difference between them?</p><p>— Today we will learn what an atom, a molecule and an ion are.</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3><ul><li><b>ATOM</b> — a particle made of a positively charged nucleus and negatively charged electrons. Examples: He, Ne, Fe.</li><li><b>MOLECULE</b> — a particle formed when two or more atoms join together. Examples: H₂O, O₂, CO₂.</li><li><b>ION</b> — a charged particle that has lost or gained electrons. Examples: Na⁺, Cl⁻, Ca²⁺.</li></ul>
<h3>Compare</h3><table><tr><th></th><th>Atom</th><th>Molecule</th><th>Ion</th></tr><tr><td>Made of</td><td>One atom</td><td>Several atoms</td><td>A charged particle</td></tr><tr><td>Charge</td><td>Neutral</td><td>Neutral</td><td>Positive or negative</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>He, Br</td><td>H₂O, O₂</td><td>Na⁺, Cl⁻</td></tr></table>
<div class="box"><b>Did you know? Remember!</b> All substances are made of atoms, molecules or ions. The particles cannot be seen, but substances exist because of them.</div>`,
`<h3>Yes or no?</h3><ol><li>A molecule is made of several atoms.</li><li>An ion is a particle with a charge.</li><li>Every atom is an ion.</li><li>Water is a molecule.</li><li>Na⁺ is a positive ion.</li><li>Cl⁻ is a negative ion.</li></ol>
<p><b>Sort into the correct group (atom / molecule / ion):</b> He, O₂, Na⁺, CO₂, Cl⁻, Fe.</p>
<h3>STEM corner</h3><p>Use coloured balls to build these models: 1) a single atom (neon atom); 2) a water molecule (H₂O); 3) a Na⁺ ion.</p><p><b>Question:</b> Which model contains only one atom?</p>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>Ions in our body such as Na⁺, K⁺ and Ca²⁺ are very important for passing nerve impulses, contracting muscles and keeping the heart working.</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Every particle, however small, has its own job. Just as every person matters in a team, every particle has its place in nature.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write the differences between an atom, a molecule and an ion.</li><li>Give 3 examples of each.</li><li>Sort into groups: O₂, I⁻, Na⁺, H₂O, Fe, Cl⁻, He.</li></ol>
<div class="box g"><b>What did we learn today?</b> What is an atom? What is a molecule? What is an ion? How do they differ? I could give examples. If we understand tiny particles, we can make great discoveries!</div>`];

P[6] = [`<h2>States of matter</h2>
<p>✔ The difference between the three states &nbsp; ✔ Three states of one substance</p>
<p><b>Key question:</b> Why are ice, water and steam considered one substance even though they look different? ICE (solid) — WATER (liquid) — STEAM (gas).</p>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>Teacher Kamol showed three things on the table: ice, water and steam.</p><p>— Children, do you think these are three different substances?</p><p>Po'lat: — No, they all look like water.</p><p>— Correct! Today we will get to know the states of matter.</p></div>
<h3>Did you know?</h3>
<div class="box"><b>1. SOLID STATE</b> ✔ Has its own shape. ✔ Its volume does not change. ✔ Its particles are packed very tightly. Examples: ice, stone, iron.</div>
<div class="box"><b>2. LIQUID STATE</b> ✔ Keeps its volume. ✔ Takes the shape of its container. ✔ Its particles slide past each other. Examples: water, milk, oil.</div>
<div class="box"><b>3. GAS STATE</b> ✔ Has neither its own shape nor volume. ✔ Fills the whole container. ✔ Its particles move freely. Examples: air, oxygen, CO₂ (carbon dioxide).</div>
<h3>Compare</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Solid</th><th>Liquid</th><th>Gas</th></tr><tr><td>Shape</td><td>Own shape</td><td>Takes the container's shape</td><td>No own shape</td></tr><tr><td>Volume</td><td>Does not change</td><td>Kept</td><td>Not kept (fills the container)</td></tr><tr><td>Particle arrangement</td><td>Very tight</td><td>Close, but sliding</td><td>Far apart, moving freely</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Ice, stone, iron</td><td>Water, milk, oil</td><td>Air, oxygen, CO₂</td></tr></table>
<p>Ice, water and steam are three states of one substance (H₂O): ice ⇄ water (heating / cooling) ⇄ steam (heating / cooling).</p>`,
`<h3>Yes or no?</h3><ol><li>Ice is in the solid state.</li><li>Water has its own shape.</li><li>A gas fills every part of its container.</li><li>Ice and water are different substances.</li><li>A liquid takes the shape of its container.</li><li>Steam is a gas.</li></ol>
<p><b>Put each into the correct basket:</b> ice, milk, air, oil, stone, water, steam, helium.</p>
<h3>STEM corner: "One substance — three states"</h3><p>Draw the arrangement of particles in the empty glasses: 1. Ice (solid) 2. Water (liquid) 3. Steam (gas).</p><p><b>Question:</b> How do particles change when heated and when cooled?</p>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>In hospitals, medicines are used as solids (tablets), liquids (syrups) and gases (oxygen). That is why knowing the states of matter is important in medicine too.</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>One substance can exist in different states. A person who can adapt to different situations also achieves success.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Find 5 substances each in the solid, liquid and gas states at home.</li><li>Explain with a drawing how ice turns into water and water into steam.</li><li>Complete the table: substance — state (air, stone, steam…).</li></ol>
<h3>Who finds it fastest?</h3><p>In the pictures, mark gases green, liquids blue and solids red: ice, water, oil, spoon, steam, milk, stone, air inside a balloon, a cloud.</p>
<div class="box g"><b>What did we learn today?</b> What is a state of matter? How many states are there? In which states is the shape kept? How does particle arrangement differ?</div>`];

P[7] = [`<h2>Melting and boiling points of substances</h2>
<p><b>Key question:</b> Why does every substance melt at a particular temperature and boil at a particular temperature?</p>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>Teacher Kamol put an ice cube in a glass and heated it.</p><p>— Where did this ice disappear to?</p><p>— When it is warm, it melts! — said Asal.</p><p>— And after that? — asked Po'lat.</p><p>— If we heat it more, the water turns into steam! — said Kumush.</p><p>— Correct! Today we will learn about the melting and boiling points of substances.</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3><ul><li><b>MELTING POINT</b> — the exact temperature at which a solid begins to turn into a liquid under the effect of heat. Example: ice melts at 0 °C.</li><li><b>BOILING POINT</b> — the temperature at which a liquid turns vigorously into a gas throughout its whole volume. Example: water boils at 100 °C.</li></ul>
<h3>Compare</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Melting point</th><th>Boiling point</th></tr><tr><td>Change of state</td><td>Solid → liquid</td><td>Liquid → gas</td></tr><tr><td>When does it happen?</td><td>While the substance melts</td><td>While the substance boils</td></tr><tr><td>Volume</td><td>Part of the solid melts and becomes liquid</td><td>Vaporisation happens throughout the liquid</td></tr><tr><td>Example (water)</td><td>0 °C</td><td>100 °C</td></tr></table>
<div class="box"><b>Did you know?</b> Every substance has its own melting and boiling points. They depend on the nature of the substance, its composition and the external pressure.</div>
<p><b>Change of state (water):</b> SOLID (ice) → heating → LIQUID (water) → boiling at 100 °C → GAS (steam); cooling reverses the process.</p>`,
`<h3>Yes or no?</h3><ol><li>Ice melts at 0 °C.</li><li>Water boils at 100 °C.</li><li>At the boiling point only part of the liquid starts to evaporate.</li><li>The melting point is the point at which a substance melts.</li><li>All substances have the same melting and boiling points.</li><li>At the boiling point the liquid turns completely into gas.</li></ol>
<p><b>Complete the table using the data:</b> water (0 °C / 100 °C), oxygen O₂, ethanol, iron Fe (melts at 1535 °C).</p>
<h3>STEM corner</h3><p>The state of a substance changes with temperature. Analyse the graph (temperature °C vs. time, min) and answer:</p><ol><li>Which part of the graph shows ice melting?</li><li>In which part is the water liquid?</li><li>In which part is the water boiling?</li><li>What happens after 100 °C?</li></ol>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>Knowing melting and boiling points matters in medicine. For example: the melting point is important for storing medicines; injection solutions are sterilised by boiling at 100 °C; oxygen is given to patients as a gas.</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Everything in nature follows its own laws. When temperature changes, the state changes. Through knowledge and experience we also learn to adapt to different situations.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Define melting point and boiling point.</li><li>Complete the table.</li><li>Find and write the melting and boiling points of water, iron and oxygen.</li></ol>
<div class="box g"><b>What did we learn today?</b> What is the melting point? What is the boiling point? At what °C does water boil? If temperature changes, does the state of a substance change?</div>`];

P[8] = [`<h2>Mixtures and solutions</h2>
<p><b>Key question:</b> Which products in a shop are pure substances, and which are mixtures or solutions?</p>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>Asal held up a juice box: — Teacher, juice is a substance too, isn't it?</p><p>— Correct.</p><p>Jasur showed a bottle of mineral water: — And mineral water?</p><p>— A very good question. Today we will uncover the secrets of shop products!</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3><ul><li><b>1. PURE SUBSTANCE</b> — a substance whose composition is always the same. Examples: distilled water, oxygen, iron, gold.</li><li><b>2. MIXTURE</b> — a system formed when two or more substances are combined without reacting chemically. Examples: soil, air, milk, granite.</li><li><b>3. SOLUTION</b> — a mixture formed when one substance spreads evenly through another. Examples: salt water, sugar water, mineral water, vinegar.</li></ul>
<h3>Compare</h3><table><tr><th></th><th>Pure substance</th><th>Mixture</th><th>Solution</th></tr><tr><td>Definition</td><td>Composition is always the same</td><td>Two or more substances combined</td><td>A uniform mixture; substances spread evenly</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Oxygen (O₂), iron, distilled water (H₂O)</td><td>Soil, air, milk, granite</td><td>Salt water, sugar water, mineral water, vinegar</td></tr><tr><td>Property</td><td>Unchanging, uniform</td><td>Variable composition; can be separated by eye</td><td>Variable composition, but parts not visible</td></tr></table>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> Every solution is a mixture, but not every mixture is a solution.</div>
<p><b>How is salt water — a solution — formed?</b> Salt (NaCl) + water (H₂O) → stirring → salt water (solution).</p>`,
`<h3>Yes or no?</h3><ol><li>Air is a mixture.</li><li>Salt water is a solution.</li><li>Distilled water is a mixture.</li><li>Milk is considered a mixture.</li><li>Every solution is a mixture.</li><li>Iron is a pure substance.</li></ol>
<p><b>Sort these products into groups:</b> juice, gold, mineral water…</p>
<h3>STEM corner: "Home laboratory"</h3><p>Take two glasses of water. Glass 1: add 1 teaspoon of sugar and stir. Glass 2: add 1 teaspoon of sand and stir.</p><p><b>Observe and answer:</b> 1. In which glass did the substance disappear completely? 2. In which glass did the substance sink to the bottom? 3. In which glass did a solution form? 4. In which glass did a mixture form?</p>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>In hospitals, physiological saline (0.9% NaCl solution) given through a drip is very important. It helps keep the body's water and salt balance.</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Sometimes different things together give a new and useful result. When people cooperate, they too achieve great success.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Find and write 5 mixtures at home.</li><li>Write 3 examples of solutions.</li><li>Complete the table (pure substance / mixture): salt, milk, mineral water, air, oxygen.</li></ol>
<p><b>Colour each product in the correct group</b> (pure substance / mixture / solution): salt, juice, mineral water, vegetable oil, honey, vinegar, sugar, flour, fizzy drink, tea, gold jewellery.</p>
<div class="box g"><b>What did we learn today?</b> Which products in a grocery shop are solutions?</div>`];

P[9] = [`<h2>Methods of separating mixtures</h2>
<p><b>The filtration method — hospital laboratory</b></p>
<p><b>Key question:</b> How is a solid separated from a mixture of a solid and a liquid? (mud + water)</p>
<div class="dlg"><p>Jasur watched an experiment in the hospital laboratory.</p><p>— Teacher, how are you separating the mud from this muddy water?</p><p>— This is the filtration method, — said Teacher Kamol.</p><p>— So we can leave the mud in one place and pass the water to another!</p><p>This method is widely used in many places, especially in water purification and medicine.</p></div>
<h3>Filtration method</h3><p>If a solid does not dissolve in a liquid, the mixture is passed through filter paper: the solid stays on the filter and the liquid passes through and collects in a separate container.</p>
<p><b>Used:</b> in purifying drinking water; in industry to clean solutions; in preparing medicinal solutions; in lab work to separate solids.</p>
<h3>Comparison table</h3><table><tr><th>Method</th><th>Filtration</th><th>Distillation</th><th>Dialysis</th></tr><tr><td>What is separated</td><td>Undissolved solid + liquid</td><td>Liquid + liquid (with dissolved substances)</td><td>Very tiny particles and ions in a solution</td></tr><tr><td>Main rule</td><td>Passed through filter paper</td><td>Separated by boiling and evaporating</td><td>Separated through a semi-permeable membrane</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Sand + water</td><td>Pure water from salt water</td><td>Blood dialysis</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment</td><td>Filter paper, funnel, glass</td><td>Boiler, condenser, flask</td><td>Dialysis machine, semi-permeable membrane</td></tr></table>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> In filtration the solid must be undissolved. The holes in filter paper are smaller than the solid particles. The liquid passes through the filter; the solid stays.</div>
<p><b>Stages of filtration:</b> 1. Pour the mixture into the funnel. 2. Filter paper is in place. 3. The solid stays on the filter. 4. The clean liquid passes into a separate container.</p>`,
`<h3>Yes or no?</h3><ol><li>In filtration the solid stays on the filter.</li><li>In distillation the vapour of a liquid is cooled and turns back into a liquid.</li><li>Dialysis uses a semi-permeable membrane.</li><li>Filtration is used to separate a mixture of two liquids.</li><li>Distillation is used only for dissolved substances.</li><li>Dialysis is not used in medicine.</li></ol>
<h3>STEM corner: "A mini filtration experiment at home"</h3><p><b>Equipment:</b> a glass, a funnel, filter paper, sand, water.</p><ol><li>Make a mixture of sand and water in a glass.</li><li>Place the filter paper in the funnel.</li><li>Slowly pass the mixture through the filter.</li><li>What did you observe? Where did the solid stay and where did the liquid go?</li></ol>
<h3>Problem and solution</h3><p>Which method would you choose? Write why: 1. A factory must clean a mixture of mud and water. 2. Drinking water must be obtained from sea water. 3. The blood of a patient with kidney failure must be cleaned.</p>
<h3>Think and discuss</h3><p>You are given three mixtures. Plan which method you will use for each and write the steps. Tick the correct method (filtration / distillation / dialysis) for: soil + water, salt water, blood (in medicine), paint + water, sand + water.</p>
<h3>Medical corner — dialysis</h3><p>Dialysis is a way of cleaning the blood of patients whose kidneys no longer work properly. <b>Worldwide:</b> more than 3 million people need dialysis every year, and the number is growing.</p><p><b>Causes of this disease:</b> diabetes; high blood pressure (hypertension); chronic kidney inflammation; poor diet and lifestyle; misuse of medicines; hereditary factors. Protect your kidneys with a healthy lifestyle!</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write examples of filtration, distillation and dialysis.</li><li>Complete the table: method — how it works — which mixture — equipment.</li><li>Find 3 examples of filtration at home or around you and write them.</li></ol>`];
})(PAGES_TR.g6);
(function (P) {
P[10] = [`<h2>Chemical reactions</h2>
<p><b>Key question:</b> What happens when substances interact with each other?</p>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>Teacher Kamol poured vinegar into a container. Then he began adding soda with a spoon. Suddenly… — Fizz! — there was a sound and it foamed!</p><p>— Teacher, what happened?</p><p>— This is a chemical reaction, children! Look, a new substance — carbon dioxide gas — is forming!</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3>
<p><b>1. What is a chemical reaction?</b> When one or more substances interact and form a new substance or substances, it is called a chemical reaction. (Vinegar CH₃COOH + soda → foaming — CO₂ gas forms.) <b>Chemical reaction — a new substance forms.</b></p>
<p><b>2. Chemical equation.</b> Writing a chemical reaction with letters and formulas is called a chemical equation.</p>
<p><b>3. Parts of a reaction equation.</b> A chemical equation has three parts: <b>reactants</b> (substances that react) → the reaction arrow (means "forms") → <b>products</b> (substances formed).</p>
<div class="box">CH₃COOH + NaHCO₃ → CO₂ + H₂O + CH₃COONa</div>
<h3>Signs that a reaction is taking place</h3><ol><li>Gas is released (bubbles, sound).</li><li>Heat or light is given out.</li><li>Colour changes.</li><li>A precipitate forms.</li><li>The smell changes (or a new smell appears).</li></ol>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> ✔ A chemical reaction produces a new substance or substances. ✔ A chemical equation shows the reaction with formulas. ✔ You can tell a reaction is happening by 5 signs.</div>
<table><tr><th>Process</th><th>What happens?</th><th>New substance?</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Physical change</td><td>State or shape changes, but composition does not</td><td>No</td><td>Ice melting, salt dissolving</td></tr><tr><td>Chemical reaction</td><td>Substances interact and new substances form</td><td>Yes</td><td>Vinegar + soda → CO₂ gas</td></tr></table>`,
`<h3>Yes or no?</h3><ol><li>A chemical reaction produces a new substance.</li><li>A physical change produces a new substance.</li><li>Gas release is one of the signs of a chemical reaction.</li><li>A change of colour of a solution can show a reaction.</li><li>Ice melting is a chemical reaction.</li><li>A chemical equation is always read from left to right.</li></ol>
<p><b>Which of these are chemical reactions? Tick and give the sign:</b> iron rusting, paper burning, salt dissolving in water, milk turning sour, table salt evaporating.</p>
<h3>STEM corner: "Let's watch gas form!"</h3><p><b>Equipment:</b> vinegar, soda, a glass/bottle, a tablespoon, a balloon. Pour vinegar into the bottle, put 2 tablespoons of soda into the balloon, fit the balloon over the bottle and watch the change. Record your observations.</p>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>Chemical reactions are very important for our life and health. For example: during digestion nutrients are broken down; medicines act in the body; antibiotics react chemically with bacteria and destroy them.</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Chemical reactions take place every day in nature, in our homes and in our bodies. By observing the events around us, we understand science.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write the definition and signs of a chemical reaction in your notebook.</li><li>Write these reactions as chemical equations: a) zinc + hydrochloric acid → zinc chloride + hydrogen; b) magnesium + oxygen → magnesium oxide; c) aluminium + copper(II) sulfate → aluminium sulfate + copper.</li><li>Find 3 examples of chemical reactions around you and write their signs.</li></ol>
<h3>Think and discuss!</h3><p>Decide whether the processes shown in the pictures are chemical or physical changes and give reasons: 1. What kind of event is it? 2. Which sign is observed? 3. Was a new substance formed?</p>`];

P[11] = [`<h2>Combustion reactions</h2>
<div class="box y"><b>Fire safety rules:</b> never leave an open flame unattended! Put out a campfire before you leave!</div>
<p><b>Key question:</b> Why do some substances burn while others do not? What is the correct thing to do if a fire breaks out?</p>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>During an excursion, Teacher Kamol took the children to a special recreation area. There, following safety rules, a small campfire was lit.</p><p>Jasur asked in surprise: — Teacher, why did the wood suddenly catch fire?</p><p>Teacher Kamol smiled and answered: — Because the conditions needed for burning are present. Let's discover this secret together today.</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3>
<p><b>1. Combustion reaction.</b> Combustion is a chemical reaction in which a substance reacts quickly with oxygen, giving out heat and often light.</p>
<p><b>2. What does burning need? — the fire triangle:</b> fuel (e.g. wood), oxygen (air) and heat (temperature). If any one of the three factors is removed, burning stops.</p>
<p><b>3. How can a fire be put out?</b> With water (not for every fire); with a fire extinguisher; by blocking the supply of oxygen.</p>
<h3>Compare</h3><table><tr><th>Physical change</th><th>Chemical change</th></tr><tr><td>Ice melting, water evaporating, salt dissolving</td><td>Wood burning, a candle burning, paper burning</td></tr></table>
<div class="box"><b>Remember!</b> Combustion is a chemical reaction, because new substances (for example carbon dioxide and ash) are formed. Main products of combustion: carbon dioxide (CO₂), water (H₂O) and other substances.</div>
<p><b>Signs of combustion:</b> gas release (smoke, gas); heat and light; colour change; formation of residue; change of smell.</p>`,
`<p><b>Which of these are combustion reactions?</b> A candle burning, ice melting, wood burning, an egg cooking, a gas stove burning.</p>
<h3>Yes or no?</h3><ol><li>Combustion is a chemical reaction.</li><li>Burning needs oxygen.</li><li>Any fire can be put out with water.</li><li>A fire extinguisher helps put out a fire.</li><li>New substances form when a candle burns.</li><li>Heat is released during combustion.</li></ol>
<h3>STEM corner: "Why does the candle go out?"</h3><p><b>Equipment:</b> a candle, a glass beaker. 1. Light the candle. 2. Cover it with the glass. 3. Observe.</p><p><b>Questions:</b> Why did the candle go out? Which factor ran out? Which part of the fire triangle disappeared?</p>
<h3>Medical corner — burns</h3><p>Every year millions of people around the world suffer burns. Main causes: open flame, electric current, boiling water, chemicals, hot steam.</p><p><b>First aid:</b> 1. Hold the burned area under cool running water for 10–20 minutes. 2. Do not apply oil, toothpaste or other folk remedies. 3. For serious burns see a doctor immediately.</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Fire is useful, but carelessness creates great danger. A responsible person protects both nature and themselves.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 3 examples of combustion reactions.</li><li>Write 4 ways of putting out a fire.</li><li>Complete the table — chemical or not: candle burning, ice melting, wood burning, water evaporating, gas stove burning.</li></ol>
<h3>Analyse the situations</h3><p>1. A small fire in a rubbish bin. 2. Overheated oil burning in the kitchen. 3. An unattended campfire in a forest. 4. A glass is placed over a burning candle. For each: What happened? How can it be put out? Why is that method correct?</p>`];

P[12] = [`<h2>Acids and alkalis</h2>
<p><b>Key question:</b> Why is a lemon sour while soap is slippery?</p>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>Teacher Kamol put a lemon, vinegar and soap solution on the table. Asal tasted a little lemon: — Oh, it's very sour!</p><p>Po'lat felt that the soap solution made his hands slippery. — Why are their properties so different?</p><p>Teacher Kamol smiled: — Because one of them is an acid and the other is an alkali. Today we will learn the differences.</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3><table><tr><th>1. Acid</th><th>2. Alkali</th><th>3. Neutral substance</th></tr><tr><td>Substances that form hydrogen ions (H⁺) when dissolved in water. Examples: hydrochloric acid (HCl), acetic acid (CH₃COOH), citric acid.</td><td>Substances that form hydroxide ions (OH⁻) when dissolved in water. Examples: sodium hydroxide (NaOH), potassium hydroxide (KOH).</td><td>Has the properties of neither an acid nor an alkali. Example: pure water.</td></tr></table>
<h3>Acids and alkalis in our lives</h3><ul><li>HCl is found in stomach juice and helps digest food.</li><li>NaOH is used to make soap and detergents.</li><li>Acetic acid is found in vinegar.</li><li>Citric acid is found in fruit and drinks.</li></ul>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Acid</th><th>Alkali</th></tr><tr><td>Taste/feel</td><td>Sour taste</td><td>Bitter, slippery</td></tr><tr><td>Ions</td><td>Form H⁺ ions</td><td>Form OH⁻ ions</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>HCl</td><td>NaOH</td></tr></table>
<div class="box y"><b>Safety rules:</b> never taste or sniff chemicals. Wear goggles and gloves when working with substances. Wash your hands well after work.</div>`,
`<p><b>Sort into groups (acid / alkali / neutral):</b> lemon juice, vinegar, soap solution, bleach, pure water.</p>
<h3>Fill in the blanks</h3><ol><li>In water, acids form ___ ions.</li><li>In water, alkalis form ___ ions.</li><li>HCl is found in the ___.</li><li>NaOH is used in ___ products.</li><li>Pure water is a ___ substance.</li></ol>
<h3>True or false?</h3><ol><li>HCl is table salt.</li><li>NaOH is used in detergents.</li><li>Pure water is a neutral substance.</li><li>Acids form H⁺ ions.</li><li>Alkalis form OH⁻ ions.</li><li>Vinegar is an acid.</li></ol>
<h3>Medical corner: the acid in our stomach</h3><p>Stomach juice contains hydrochloric acid (HCl). It helps digest food, destroys some germs and is an important part of the digestive system. If the amount of acid rises above or falls below normal, stomach diseases may develop. That is why taking acid-reducing medicines without a doctor's advice is not recommended.</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Chemicals make human life easier, but they must be used only correctly and carefully.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 3 examples of acids.</li><li>Write 3 examples of alkalis.</li><li>Write one example each of how HCl and NaOH are used in daily life.</li></ol>
<p><b>Who uses which substance?</b> Match: doctor, soap-making engineer, factory worker, water treatment — NaOH, acetic acid, citric acid… Why is that particular substance used?</p>`];

P[13] = [`<h2>The pH scale</h2>
<p><b>Key question:</b> How can we find out whether a substance is acidic, neutral or alkaline?</p>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>Asal held a lemon and said: — Very sour!</p><p>Po'lat held the soap: — It's slippery!</p><p>Teacher Kamol smiled: — So their media are not the same. Today we will get to know the pH scale, which shows this difference.</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3><p><b>pH</b> — a scale that shows whether a substance has an acidic, neutral or alkaline medium.</p>
<table><tr><th>Acidic medium (pH &lt; 7)</th><th>Neutral (pH = 7)</th><th>Alkaline medium (pH &gt; 7)</th></tr><tr><td>Lemon, vinegar, cola, tomato and others</td><td>Pure water</td><td>Soap, toothpaste, bleach and others</td></tr></table>
<h3>pH in our lives</h3><ul><li>Lemon — acidic (pH &lt; 7)</li><li>Vinegar — acidic</li><li>Pure water — neutral (pH = 7)</li><li>Soap — alkaline</li><li>Toothpaste — alkaline (pH &gt; 7)</li><li>Bleach — alkaline (pH &gt; 7)</li></ul>
<div class="box">Just as a thermometer shows temperature, the pH scale shows whether a medium is acidic or alkaline.</div>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> The pH scale goes from 0 to 14. Below 7 — acidic; equal to 7 — neutral; above 7 — alkaline medium.</div>`,
`<h3>1. Fill in the blanks</h3><ol><li>pH shows the ___.</li><li>The pH of pure water is ___.</li><li>Lemon has a ___ medium.</li><li>Soap has a ___ medium.</li><li>The pH scale goes from ___ to ___.</li></ol>
<h3>2. Chemistry detective</h3><p>Sort into groups (acidic / neutral / alkaline): lemon, vinegar, pure water, toothpaste, bleach.</p>
<h3>3. True or false?</h3><ol><li>Pure water has a neutral medium.</li><li>Lemon has an acidic medium.</li><li>Soap has an alkaline medium.</li><li>pH shows the medium.</li><li>Bleach has an alkaline medium.</li><li>Vinegar has a neutral medium.</li></ol>
<h3>Medical corner: pH balance in the body</h3><p>The pH of stomach juice is about 1–2. It breaks down food and destroys harmful germs. The pH of blood is about 7.4, and the body always tries to keep it normal.</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Balance matters in life too. When balance is kept in nature and in the human body, healthy life continues.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 5 examples of acidic media found at home.</li><li>Write 3 examples of alkaline media.</li><li>Why is pure water called neutral?</li></ol>
<p><b>Where on the pH strip would you place it?</b> Connect the pictures to the correct place on the pH strip.</p>`];

P[14] = [`<h2>Catalysis and enzymes</h2>
<p><b>Key question:</b> Why does dough rise? What are the "helpers" that speed up chemical reactions?</p>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>The baker kneaded her dough and added yeast. After some time the dough grew bigger and rose!</p><p>Po'lat asked in surprise: — Teacher, did the dough grow by itself?</p><p>Teacher Kamol answered: — No, very small but powerful helpers worked here. Today we will meet these helpers!</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3><ul><li><b>1. CATALYST</b> — a substance that speeds up a chemical reaction but is not itself used up.</li><li><b>2. ENZYME</b> — a natural catalyst found in living organisms that speeds up chemical reactions.</li></ul>
<p>Without a catalyst the reaction is slow; with a catalyst it is fast. Dough + yeast (enzymes) → risen dough. Milk + enzymes → yoghurt.</p>
<div class="box"><b>A CATALYST IS A HELPER, BUT IT DOES NOT CHANGE!</b> ✔ The reaction speeds up. ✔ The catalyst does not change and is not used up. ✔ Catalysts are used in factories, cars and laboratories (car catalytic converter; chemical plants; experiments; found in nature too).</div>
<div class="box"><b>AN ENZYME IS A NATURAL CATALYST.</b> ✔ Enzymes work in living organisms. ✔ Even in tiny amounts they do a lot of work. ✔ Enzymes are used in baking bread, digestion, and making yoghurt and cheese.</div>
<h3>Enzymes in our lives</h3><p>Baking bread (yeast); making yoghurt; making cheese; digesting food; tenderising meat (pineapple, papaya).</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> Catalysts and enzymes speed up chemical reactions but are not used up in the reaction. Enzymes are natural catalysts working in living organisms.</div>`,
`<p><b>Which happens faster? Tick and give the reason</b> (e.g. finely ground flour…).</p>
<p><b>Link these processes to "enzyme" or "catalyst":</b> making yoghurt, cleaning car exhaust gases, tenderising meat (papaya), reactions in a chemical plant, making cheese. ENZYME (natural catalyst) / CATALYST (artificial or material).</p>
<h3>True or false?</h3><ol><li>A catalyst slows a reaction down.</li><li>A catalyst is itself used up.</li><li>Enzymes are found in living organisms.</li><li>Yeast is an enzyme.</li><li>Enzymes work in digestion.</li><li>A car catalytic converter reduces harmful gases.</li></ol>
<h3>Fill in the blanks</h3><ol><li>A catalyst ___ a chemical reaction but is not itself used up.</li><li>Enzymes are found in ___ organisms.</li><li>Yeast acts as a ___ in baking bread.</li><li>Enzymes do work in very ___ amounts.</li><li>Catalysts are used more in ___ and laboratories.</li></ol>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>After we eat, enzymes in the stomach and intestines break food into small parts. Without enzymes, food would not be digested. Enzymes do very important work in the body, and their balance is necessary for our health.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>What is a catalyst? Explain with an example.</li><li>Write 3 examples of enzymes.</li><li>Give 2 everyday processes in which enzymes are used.</li></ol>
<p><b>Think:</b> If there were no catalysts or enzymes, how would our lives change? Write your ideas.</p>
<h3>Life lesson: Small help — big result!</h3><p>Like enzymes and catalysts, we too can cause big changes by helping others.</p>`];
})(PAGES_TR.g6);
(function (P) {
P[15] = [`<h2>Water — the source of life</h2>
<p><b>Key question:</b> Why is water called the source of life? <i>There is no life without water! Every drop of water is precious.</i></p>
<h3>Key concepts</h3>
<p><b>1. Water — the source of life.</b> Water is a substance necessary for the life of all living organisms. A person can live several weeks without food, but only a few days without water.</p>
<p><b>2. The importance of water:</b> drinking water, farming, industry, medicine, electricity, hygiene.</p>
<p><b>3. Properties of water:</b> colourless; odourless; tasteless; shapeless; it is a liquid; a very good solvent.</p>
<h3>The water cycle — always moving in nature</h3><p>Water source → purification (water treatment) → delivery to homes → use → return to nature.</p>
<div class="box"><b>Did you know?</b> About 71% of the Earth's surface is covered by water. But fresh water fit for drinking is very scarce — only about 2.5%!</div>`,
`<p><b>Where is water used?</b> Put a ✓ on the right pictures: taking a shower, farming (irrigation), hospital, industry (factories), cooking, putting out fires, producing electricity, washing a car.</p>
<h3>Fill in the blanks</h3><ol><li>Water is a ___, odourless and tasteless substance.</li><li>Because water is a good ___, many substances dissolve in it.</li><li>About ___ of the Earth's surface is covered with water.</li><li>About ___ of the human body is water.</li><li>Without water there is no ___.</li></ol>
<h3>True or false?</h3><ol><li>Water is colourless, odourless and tasteless.</li><li>Water is a very good solvent.</li><li>Life is possible without water.</li><li>All water is safe to drink.</li><li>Water should be saved.</li></ol>
<p><b>Which of these three waters is safe to drink? Give the reason:</b> 1. Tap water 2. Spring water 3. Muddy water.</p>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>About 60–70% of the human body is water. It carries nutrients, regulates body temperature and helps remove waste. That is why drinking enough water every day is very important for our health!</p>
<p><b>Water savers! Right (✓) or wrong (✗)? Why?</b> Leaving the tap running; watering the garden as needed; washing a car with drinking water; repairing a leaking tap.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 10 places at home where water is used.</li><li>Write 5 ways to save water.</li><li>Write 5–6 sentences on "A day without water".</li></ol>`];

P[16] = [`<h2>Water purity and pollution</h2>
<p><b>Key question:</b> Why is some water clean while other water is polluted?</p>
<div class="dlg"><p>During an excursion the children came to a riverbank. On one side the water was very clear. On the other, rubbish was floating.</p><p>Po'lat: — Who polluted the water?</p><p>Teacher Kamol: — We must protect nature as well. Let's find out the reasons together.</p></div>
<div class="box y"><b>Interesting fact:</b> one drop of used oil can pollute hundreds of litres of water.</div>
<p><b>The chain of water pollution:</b> rubbish → river → fish → people.</p>
<h3>Key concepts</h3><ol><li><b>Clean water</b> — water fit for drinking, free of harmful substances and germs.</li><li><b>Water pollution</b> — harmful substances get into water and its quality gets worse.</li><li><b>What pollutes water?</b> household waste, factory waste, fertilisers and pesticides, oil products, sewage.</li><li><b>Why is water important?</b> drinking, cooking, hygiene, medicine, farming, industry.</li></ol>
<div class="box"><b>Did you know?</b> Clean drinking water is a very small part of all the water on Earth. That is why it is important to value every drop.</div>`,
`<p><b>Sort the pictures into two groups</b> (polluting water / protecting water): household waste, factory waste, oil products, washing a car in the river — turning off the tap, collecting rubbish properly, planting trees, purifying water.</p>
<h3>Who is doing the right thing?</h3><p>Look at the pictures. Mark right (✓) or wrong (✗) and write why: throwing rubbish in the bin; throwing rubbish in the river; washing a car in the river; planting trees.</p>
<h3>Fill in the blanks</h3><ol><li>Water fit for drinking is called ___ water.</li><li>Harmful substances that enter water make it ___.</li><li>Clean water is necessary for people.</li><li>Factory waste can pollute rivers and lakes.</li><li>Every person must ___ water.</li></ol>
<h3>True or false?</h3><ol><li>Clean water is needed only for drinking.</li><li>Diseases can spread through polluted water.</li><li>Water will never run out.</li><li>Polluting water harms nature.</li><li>Protecting water is the duty of each of us.</li></ol>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>Some diseases can spread through dirty water: intestinal infections; poisoning; some parasitic diseases. So if you are not sure about the quality of drinking water, boil or purify it before drinking.</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Protecting water means protecting not only nature but also human health. Every drop of water is a priceless treasure for the future.</p>
<h3>Ecological patrol</h3><p>Find 10 ecological mistakes in the picture and say how they can be corrected.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 5 factors that pollute water.</li><li>Write 5 ways to save water.</li><li>Write 5–6 sentences on "Protecting water means protecting the future".</li></ol>`];

P[17] = [`<h2>The composition of air</h2>
<p><b>Key question:</b> Do you think this container is empty? What is invisible air made of?</p>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>Jasur lifted an empty glass container: — Teacher, there is nothing in this container.</p><p>Teacher Kamol smiled: — Actually it isn't empty. It contains the air we breathe. Today we will learn the composition of air.</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3>
<p><b>1. What is air?</b> Air is the mixture of gases that makes up the Earth's atmosphere.</p>
<p><b>2. Composition of air:</b> nitrogen (N₂) 78%, oxygen (O₂) 21%, other gases 1% (argon, carbon dioxide, water vapour and others).</p>
<p><b>3. Jobs of the gases:</b> oxygen — needed for breathing; nitrogen — makes up the main part of air; carbon dioxide (CO₂) — used by plants for photosynthesis.</p>
<p><b>4. Why is air important?</b> breathing; plant life; climate and weather; flight and movement.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Interesting fact:</b> one person breathes about 10–15 thousand litres of air a day!</div>
<p><b>Gas exchange in air:</b> trees give out oxygen (O₂); people and animals breathe in oxygen and give out carbon dioxide (CO₂); trees use carbon dioxide.</p>
<div class="box"><b>Did you know?</b> To have clean air, it is very important to protect trees, plants and nature!</div>`,
`<p><b>Match the gases with their jobs:</b> oxygen (O₂) — needed for breathing; nitrogen (N₂) — makes up the main part of air; carbon dioxide — used by plants for photosynthesis.</p>
<h3>What happens if there is no air?</h3><p>Look at the pictures: a burning candle, a child (a person), a bird. Which could not live or work without air? Why?</p>
<h3>Fill in the blanks</h3><ol><li>Air is a ___.</li><li>About ___ of the air is nitrogen.</li><li>Oxygen is necessary for ___.</li><li>Plants use ___ gas.</li><li>Air cannot be seen with the ___.</li></ol>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>When we breathe in, oxygen enters the lungs and is carried by the blood to all cells. That is why walking in fresh air is very good for health.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Interesting fact:</b> the Earth's atmosphere weighs billions of tonnes; it protects the Earth's surface and supports life.</div>
<h3>Can we see air?</h3><p>Which pictures prove that air exists? Tick (✓): a balloon flying, a parachute coming down, a flag waving, a pinwheel spinning. Explain your answer.</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Clean air is one of the main conditions for a healthy life. By planting trees and protecting nature, we also protect the air.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write the three main components of air.</li><li>Why is oxygen necessary?</li><li>How do trees benefit the air?</li></ol>`];

P[18] = [`<h2>Oxygen and carbon dioxide</h2>
<p><i>Gases are an important part of the cycle of life in nature.</i></p>
<div class="dlg"><p>— Teacher, where does the oxygen we breathe come from?</p><p>Today we will learn about the amazing cycle of gases: Sun → plants → oxygen → people and animals → carbon dioxide → plants.</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3>
<p><b>1. Oxygen</b> — a colourless, odourless gas necessary for the breathing of people and animals.</p>
<p><b>2. Carbon dioxide</b> — a colourless gas formed when people and animals breathe out. Plants use it in photosynthesis.</p>
<p><b>3. Where is oxygen produced?</b> Green plants release oxygen during photosynthesis in the presence of sunlight: trees, grass, other green plants.</p>
<p><b>4. The cycle of gases:</b> carbon dioxide → plants → oxygen → …</p>
<div class="box"><b>Did you know?</b> Oxygen and carbon dioxide play an important role in keeping the balance of life in nature. Their cycle continues without stopping.</div>
<div class="box y"><b>Interesting fact!</b> If there were no plants, oxygen on Earth would gradually decrease and the life of living organisms would be in danger.</div>`,
`<h3>Chemistry detective</h3><p>Match the source and use in the pictures: a tree — gives out O₂; a person breathing — gives out CO₂; car exhaust — CO₂ is released; plants in photosynthesis — release oxygen.</p>
<h3>Fill in the blanks</h3><ol><li>Oxygen is necessary for people and animals to ___.</li><li>Carbon dioxide is given out by people when they ___.</li><li>Plants use ___ in photosynthesis.</li><li>Oxygen is released mainly by ___.</li><li>The cycle of gases keeps balance in nature.</li></ol>
<h3>Match the gases</h3><p>Mark the right gas for each process: released by plants; used by plants; breathed in; breathed out; takes part in burning; formed in burning.</p>
<h3>True or false?</h3><ol><li>Plants give out CO₂ when they breathe.</li><li>Humans cannot live without oxygen.</li><li>Oxygen is used up in photosynthesis.</li><li>Cars release CO₂.</li><li>The cycle of gases is a natural process.</li></ol>
<h3>Nature corner: Why do we plant trees?</h3><p>Every tree planted means more oxygen and cleaner air for the future: more trees → more oxygen → cleaner air → healthy life.</p>
<h3>Chemistry and life: what happens when we breathe?</h3><p>Oxygen enters the body → it helps produce energy in cells → carbon dioxide is carried out → it leaves when we breathe out.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 2 reasons why oxygen is important.</li><li>Write where carbon dioxide is formed.</li><li>Explain why plants are important.</li></ol>`];

P[19] = [`<h2>Proteins, fats, carbohydrates</h2>
<p><b>Key question:</b> What do foods give our body and why are they needed?</p>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>At lunch time the children asked the teacher questions.</p><p>Jasur: — Teacher, why do athletes eat a lot of eggs?</p><p>Asal: — Why does bread give us strength?</p><p>Po'lat: — Is fat useful or harmful?</p><p>Teacher Kamol: — Today we will uncover what foods are made of and learn the job of each in our body.</p></div>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> Proteins, fats and carbohydrates are the main types of nutrients. It is very important for the body to have all three in the right amounts. HEALTHY FOOD — HEALTHY LIFE!</div>
<table><tr><th>Proteins</th><th>Carbohydrates</th><th>Fats</th></tr><tr><td>Help muscles grow and heal. <i>Building material for the body.</i></td><td>The body's main source of energy. <i>Give strength and energy.</i></td><td>Store energy, keep the body warm and help absorb some vitamins. <i>Store energy.</i></td></tr><tr><td>Examples: eggs, meat, milk, cheese, fish, beans.</td><td>Examples: rice, potatoes, bread, corn.</td><td>Examples: butter, nuts, vegetable oil, sunflower oil.</td></tr></table>
<div class="box"><b>Interesting information:</b> in a healthy diet, about 50–60% of daily food energy comes from carbohydrates.</div>`,
`<h3>Chemistry detective</h3><p>Sort these foods into groups (protein / fat / carbohydrate): egg, bread, butter, milk, rice, meat, nuts, potato, fish, banana.</p>
<h3>Analyse the dish!</h3><p>Which nutrients are in these dishes? (put ✓ for protein, fat, carbohydrate) — e.g. cheese.</p>
<h3>Fill your plate!</h3><p>Using these foods, prepare a healthy lunch plate. It must contain proteins, fats and carbohydrates.</p>
<h3>Who needs what?</h3><p>Match which nutrients are especially important for each person: PROTEIN — important for growth and repair of the body (children); CARBOHYDRATE — needed when a lot of energy is required (athletes); FAT — protects the body and stores energy (elderly people).</p>
<h3>Analyse a one-day menu!</h3><p>In the given menu, which nutrient is too much or too little? Complete the table for breakfast, lunch and dinner: ✓ enough, — too little, (+) too much.</p>
<h3>Medical corner — the balance of nutrients is a magic triangle!</h3><ul><li>Lack of protein — growth slows and tiredness appears.</li><li>Too few carbohydrates — we get tired quickly and lose strength.</li><li>No fat at all — skin dries and vitamins are poorly absorbed. Too much fat can lead to obesity and disease.</li></ul><p><b>BALANCE — HEALTH!</b></p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 3 examples for each group (protein, fat, carbohydrate).</li><li>Write whether all three nutrients were in the meals you ate today.</li></ol>
<p><b>Fill the basket!</b> Put the right foods in the basket for a healthy family.</p>`];
})(PAGES_TR.g6);
(function (P) {
P[20] = [`<h2>Fruit and vegetables — good for health! (Vitamins and minerals)</h2>
<p><b>Key question:</b> Why are vitamins and minerals necessary for our body to be healthy?</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> Vitamins and minerals are substances that are vital for the body, even in small amounts. They protect us from disease and help us grow and work well.</div>
<h3>1. Sources of vitamins</h3><p>Vitamins help the body grow, develop and protect itself. Each vitamin has its own job and is found in different foods.</p>
<table><tr><th>Vitamin</th><th>Job</th><th>Sources</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>Improves eyesight, protects skin</td><td>Carrots, pumpkin</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>Strengthens immunity, helps wounds heal</td><td>Oranges, peppers, kiwi</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>Strengthens bones and teeth</td><td>Sunlight, fish, milk</td></tr><tr><td>B group</td><td>Helps the nervous system and energy exchange</td><td>Grains, meat, eggs</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>Protects skin and mucous membranes</td><td>Vegetable oil, avocado</td></tr></table>
<h3>Body map</h3><p>Vitamins and minerals play an important role in how different organs work: eyes — vitamin A; bones — vitamin D, Ca; blood — Fe (iron); thyroid gland — I (iodine); immunity — vitamin C; skin — A, C vitamins.</p>
<h3>2. Sources of minerals</h3><p>Minerals help many processes in the body run correctly.</p>
<table><tr><th>Mineral</th><th>Job</th><th>Sources</th></tr><tr><td>Calcium (Ca)</td><td>Strengthens bones and teeth</td><td>Milk, cheese</td></tr><tr><td>Iron (Fe)</td><td>Takes part in blood formation, prevents anaemia</td><td>Meat, liver</td></tr><tr><td>Iodine (I)</td><td>Regulates the thyroid gland</td><td>Sea fish, iodised salt</td></tr><tr><td>Magnesium (Mg)</td><td>Helps muscles and the nervous system work</td><td>Nuts, beans</td></tr><tr><td>Potassium (K)</td><td>Supports the heart and water–salt balance</td><td>Bananas, potatoes, dried apricots</td></tr></table>
<div class="box">Eat fruit and vegetables of different colours — get many different vitamins and minerals!</div>`,
`<h3>4. Fix the one-day menu!</h3><p>Is this child's food today correct? What is missing? Make a healthy menu. <i>Today's menu: crisps, hamburger…</i> Sample healthy menu: vegetables, fruit, fish, milk.</p>
<h3>6. Supermarket detective</h3><p>Tick the products rich in vitamins and minerals: kiwi, meat, broccoli, orange, nuts, eggs, chocolate…</p>
<h3>8. What happens if a vitamin is missing?</h3><p>Read the symptoms and write which vitamin or mineral helps: gets tired a lot, low immunity, often catches colds; bones ache, teeth break easily; eyesight got worse, cannot see well in the dark; tires quickly, feels dizzy.</p>
<h3>Read the food label</h3><p>Nutritional content (per 100 g): calcium (Ca) 120 mg; vitamin D 1.5 mcg; vitamin B12 0.4 mcg; vitamin C 4 mg. 1. Which vitamins are in this product? 2. Which minerals? 3. Which organs and processes does it benefit?</p>
<table><tr><th>If missing</th><th>Problem</th><th>Sources</th></tr><tr><td>Vitamin A</td><td>Eyesight gets worse, skin becomes dry</td><td>Carrots, pumpkin, egg yolk, milk</td></tr><tr><td>Vitamin D</td><td>Bones weaken; rickets may develop</td><td>Fish, eggs, milk, sunlight</td></tr><tr><td>Fe (iron)</td><td>Anaemia, pale skin, tiredness</td><td>Meat, liver, green vegetables</td></tr><tr><td>I (iodine)</td><td>Affects growth</td><td>Iodised salt, sea fish</td></tr><tr><td>Vitamin C</td><td>Blood vessels weaken</td><td>Peppers, citrus fruit</td></tr></table>
<p><b>Think:</b> What would human life be like without vitamins and minerals? Write 2–3 sentences.</p>
<h3>9. Healthy choice!</h3><p>Sort these products into three groups: rich in vitamins / rich in minerals / less useful (chocolate, nuts, hamburger…).</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 5 products rich in vitamins.</li><li>Write 5 products rich in minerals.</li><li>Write your family's menu for one day and find which vitamins and minerals may be missing.</li></ol>`];

P[21] = [`<h2>Sugar and tooth decay</h2>
<p><b>Key question:</b> Why are we advised to brush our teeth after eating sweets? <b>HEALTHY TEETH — HEALTHY LIFE!</b></p>
<div class="box">Nobody "eats" your teeth. Bacteria in the mouth break down sugar and produce acid. That acid slowly destroys tooth enamel.</div>
<h3>An interesting story (comic)</h3><p>Jasur ate a lot of chocolate → he forgot to brush his teeth → his tooth hurt → the dentist explained why → so what matters is moderation and hygiene!</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> Sugar is a source of energy for the body. But eating a lot of it and not cleaning your teeth can cause tooth decay.</div>
<h3>Key concepts: how does tooth decay develop?</h3><ol><li><b>Sugar</b> — we eat sweets or sugary food.</li><li><b>Bacteria</b> — bacteria in the mouth feed on the sugar.</li><li><b>Acid</b> — bacteria break down the sugar and produce acid.</li><li><b>Enamel is destroyed</b> — acid slowly destroys tooth enamel and a hole forms.</li><li><b>Caries</b> — pain, a hole and inflammation appear in the tooth.</li></ol>
<p><b>The chemical process (simply):</b> sugar (carbohydrate) → bacteria → acid (lactic acids) → tooth enamel destroyed.</p>
<h3>3 rules that protect teeth</h3><ul><li>Brush your teeth twice a day.</li><li>Rinse your mouth after sweets.</li><li>Visit the dentist every 6 months.</li></ul>`,
`<h3>1. Who is a friend to teeth?</h3><p>Sort the products into two groups: TOOTH-FRIENDLY (useful in moderation) — carrots, cheese, milk; HARMFUL IF EATEN OFTEN — sweets, chocolate, fizzy drinks.</p>
<h3>2. A day's habits</h3><p>Look at the picture: breakfast — ate sweets — sweet drink — didn't brush teeth — brushed in the evening. Where is the mistake? Which habit needs to change? Why?</p>
<h3>3. Chemistry detective</h3><p>Tick after which products rinsing your mouth or brushing is recommended: juice, apple, fizzy drink, milk, nuts. <i>Note: after sweet and acid-forming products, rinse your mouth or brush your teeth.</i></p>
<h3>4. Observe and draw conclusions</h3><p>Clean tooth vs. decayed tooth: 1. In which picture is the enamel healthy? 2. What may have caused the decay? 3. How can tooth enamel be protected?</p>
<h3>5. Tooth laboratory</h3><p>Place the items correctly: PROTECTS TEETH (toothbrush, water) / MAY CAUSE DECAY (bacteria, sugar, acid).</p>
<h3>6. Which product has more sugar?</h3><p>Tick the products that may contain a lot of added sugar: fizzy drink, chocolate, biscuits, apple, milk.</p>
<h3>7. Life lesson</h3><p>You don't have to give up sweets completely — the right habits protect our teeth: eat sweets in moderation; don't forget to brush on time; rinse your mouth; visit the dentist regularly.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write in your notebook how tooth decay develops in 5 steps.</li><li>Write 3 tips for your friends on keeping teeth healthy.</li><li>Observe yourself on days with and without sweets and write your conclusion.</li></ol>`];

P[22] = [`<h2>Food additives</h2>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>While shopping, the children saw various labels on product packages: E300, E330, E440…</p><p>Po'lat asked in surprise: — Aren't all of these harmful substances?</p><p>Teacher Kamol answered: — No. Not all food additives are harmful. Let's find out what they do.</p></div>
<h3>What is an "E" code?</h3><p>The letter "E" is the international code of food additives. E300 — ascorbic acid (vitamin C); E330 — citric acid; E440 — pectin (obtained from fruit). <b>Having an E code does not mean a product is harmful!</b></p>
<h3>Key concept</h3><p><b>Food additive</b> — a substance added to food to improve its taste, colour, texture or shelf life.</p>
<h3>Why are additives added?</h3><ul><li><b>Colouring</b> — to improve the product's colour.</li><li><b>Extending shelf life</b> — so the product keeps longer without spoiling.</li><li><b>Improving taste</b> — to strengthen or keep the taste.</li><li><b>Thickening (improving texture)</b> — to make the product thicker and smoother.</li></ul>
<h3>Natural and artificial additives</h3><table><tr><th>Natural additives (obtained from nature)</th><th>Artificial additives (made synthetically)</th></tr><tr><td>Pectin, citric acid, vitamin C, plant extracts</td><td>Some colourings, some flavour enhancers, some preservatives</td></tr></table>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> Reading the ingredients when choosing a product is a good habit. It helps us make an informed choice.</div>`,
`<h3>1. Label detective</h3><p>Look carefully at these labels. Find the additives written with an E code and circle them.</p><ul><li><b>Yoghurt:</b> milk, cream, sugar, fruit pieces, citric acid (E330), flavouring.</li><li><b>Juice:</b> water, apple juice, sugar, ascorbic acid (E300), flavouring.</li><li><b>Biscuits:</b> wheat flour, vegetable oil, sugar, pectin (E440), salt, caramel colour (E150).</li></ul>
<h3>2. Find the job</h3><p>Match with a line: colouring — strengthens the product's colour; preservative — helps keep the product longer; flavour enhancer — strengthens taste; thickener — thickens the texture.</p>
<h3>3. Conscious shopping</h3><p>Look at the picture. For which products in the baskets is it important to read the label? Why?</p>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>In some people, certain food additives can cause allergic reactions: itchy skin and rashes, stomach ache, blocked nose and sneezing. That is why it is important to read the label, follow the doctor's advice and know the ingredients!</p>
<h3>Think: "All products with an E code are harmful." Yes or no?</h3><p>Explain your answer.</p>
<h3>6. Life lesson</h3><p>A conscious shopper reads the label before choosing a product: read the label → know the ingredients → make an informed choice → protect your health.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Look at the labels of 3 food products at home.</li><li>Write the names or codes of at least 2 additives from their ingredients.</li><li>Read a product label together with your parents and discuss why this habit is useful.</li></ol>`];

P[23] = [`<h2>How do medicines work?</h2>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>Asal caught a cold. The doctor prescribed her a syrup. Asal took the medicine, rested and recovered a few days later.</p><p>Jasur asked: — What happens in our body after we take medicine?</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3>
<p><b>1. What is a medicine?</b> A medicine is a substance or mixture of substances used to treat or prevent disease.</p>
<p><b>2. How does a medicine work?</b> An illness appears → the medicine is swallowed or used in another way → the medicine passes into the blood and reaches the right place → it acts on the cause of the illness and the body recovers.</p>
<p><b>3. Composition of a medicine.</b> Every medicine contains two kinds of substances: the <b>active substance</b> — acts on the cause of the illness; <b>excipients</b> — keep the medicine's form or make it easier to take.</p>
<p><b>4. Forms of medicine:</b> tablet — swallowed by mouth; syrup — swallowed, convenient for children; ointment — rubbed on the skin; drops — put into the eyes, ears or nose; spray — sprayed into the nose or mouth.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Important reminder!</b> Take medicines only on the advice of a doctor or your parents! Taking medicine on your own can harm your health.</div>`,
`<h3>1. Which medicine form?</h3><p>Match with a line: tablet — swallowed by mouth; syrup — swallowed, convenient for children; ointment — rubbed on skin; drops — put into the nose; spray — sprayed into the mouth.</p>
<h3>2. What does the doctor advise?</h3><p>What should be done first in each case? Tick: a child with a fever; a child with a grazed knee; a child with a sore throat — tell parents / rest / see a doctor.</p>
<h3>3. Which items are not medicines?</h3><p>Mark with ✗ the items that are not medicines: tablet, ointment, vitamin, bandage…</p>
<h3>Medical corner: how should medicines be stored?</h3><ul><li>Store medicines where children cannot reach them.</li><li>Check expiry dates regularly.</li><li>Store medicines in a dry, cool place out of sunlight.</li><li>Keep medicine in its original box; don't move it to another container.</li><li>Don't give a medicine to other people that a doctor didn't prescribe for them.</li></ul>
<h3>5. Read the instructions!</h3><p>Why must you read the leaflet before taking medicine? Tick the correct answers: to know the dose; to know when to use it; warnings about side effects are given; to swallow the medicine faster.</p>
<h3>6. Think about it! True or false?</h3><ol><li>Medicine helps treat disease.</li><li>I can decide myself to give a medicine to my friend.</li><li>If I take more medicine, I'll get well faster.</li><li>Every medicine contains an active substance.</li><li>It is important to use medicine correctly and in the right dose.</li></ol>
<h3>7. Life lesson</h3><p>Using medicines correctly is part of a healthy lifestyle. Taking medicine on your own can cause harm.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 3 forms of medicine found at home (e.g. syrup, ointment, tablet).</li><li>Write how each form is used.</li><li>Why shouldn't children take medicines by themselves?</li></ol>`];

P[24] = [`<h2>Bacteria and antibiotics</h2>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>Jasur was about to eat without washing his hands. Teacher Kamol stopped him: <b>WASH YOUR HANDS!</b></p><p>— Even if your hands look clean, they may have tiny living creatures on them. Let's look through a microscope!</p><p>That is where our research began.</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3>
<p><b>1. What is a bacterium?</b> A bacterium is a very tiny living organism that cannot be seen with the eye.</p>
<p><b>2. Not all bacteria are harmful!</b> <b>Useful bacteria:</b> take part in making yoghurt, kefir and cheese; help digest food in the intestines. <b>Disease-causing bacteria:</b> some bacteria can cause illnesses.</p>
<p><b>3. What is an antibiotic?</b> An antibiotic is a medicine used to treat diseases caused by certain bacteria.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Important reminder!</b> Antibiotics must be taken only on a doctor's advice! Taking them on your own can harm your health.</div>
<p><b>4. Why do medicines work?</b> Illness → medicine is taken → passes into the blood → reaches the right place → the body recovers.</p>
<p><b>5. Forms of medicine:</b> tablet, syrup, ointment, drops, spray.</p>
<p><b>6. Hygiene — the best protection!</b> Wash your hands; wash fruit and vegetables; cover your mouth when you cough or sneeze; air the room; stay in a clean environment.</p>`,
`<h3>1. Under the microscope</h3><p>Which of these pictures is a bacterium? Tick (✓).</p>
<h3>2. Clean hands — healthy life</h3><p>Which hand may have more bacteria? Tick: clean hand / dirty hand.</p>
<h3>3. Which is useful?</h3><p>Mark the products in which useful bacteria take part (yoghurt, cheese…).</p>
<h3>4. Find the pair</h3><p>Match: microscope — used to see very tiny organisms; antibiotic — used to treat diseases caused by some bacteria; soap — helps clean hands.</p>
<h3>5. Prevent disease!</h3><p>What do these habits help with? Write the answer: washing hands with soap; covering your mouth when coughing or sneezing; washing fruit and vegetables; airing the room. These habits help to ___.</p>
<h3>6. Which are not medicines?</h3><p>Mark with ✗: tablet, syrup, vitamin, ointment, thermometer, bandage.</p>
<h3>7. Read the leaflet</h3><p>Every medicine box contains an instruction leaflet. Why should it be read before taking the medicine?</p>
<h3>8. Think about it — true or false?</h3><ol><li>All bacteria are harmful.</li><li>An antibiotic cures any disease.</li><li>Useful bacteria help digestion.</li><li>Antibiotics may be taken without a doctor's advice.</li><li>Following hygiene rules prevents disease.</li></ol>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>There is no need to fear every bacterium. Following hygiene rules, using medicines correctly and following the doctor's advice help protect your health.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 2 examples of useful bacteria.</li><li>Write 3 ways to protect yourself from disease-causing bacteria.</li><li>Explain why a microscope is needed.</li></ol>`];
})(PAGES_TR.g6);
(function (P) {
P[25] = [`<h2>Toxic and hazardous substances</h2>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>Insects started damaging the tomato leaves in the garden. The farmer protected the plants with a special product.</p><p>Jasur asked: — If this product harms insects, doesn't it harm people too?</p><p>Teacher Kamol answered: — That is why such substances must be handled very carefully.</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3>
<p><b>Toxic substance</b> — a substance that can cause harm when it acts on the body wrongly or in excess.</p>
<p><b>Hazardous substance</b> — a substance that can harm people, animals or the environment when used incorrectly.</p>
<p><b>Pesticide</b> — a group of substances used to protect plants from pests and diseases.</p>
<p><b>Insecticide</b> — a type of pesticide used to destroy insects or reduce their number.</p>
<h3>What do pesticides protect against?</h3><p>They protect plants from pests and diseases: insects, fungi, disease-causing organisms, weeds and rodents.</p>
<h3>Do you know the safety signs?</h3><ul><li><b>Toxic substance</b> — seriously harms human health.</li><li><b>Flammable substance</b> — may cause fire.</li><li><b>Warning</b> — be careful! There may be danger.</li><li><b>Wear gloves</b> — recommended.</li><li><b>Wear eye protection</b> — recommended.</li></ul>
<h3>Protective equipment</h3><p>When working with hazardous substances it is recommended to wear protective clothing, gloves and a mask. Such equipment protects us.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Important reminder!</b> Pesticides and insecticides are used only by adults and specially trained specialists. Children must not use these substances or go near them!</div>`,
`<h3>1. Mark the protective equipment</h3><p>Tick (✓) the protective equipment in the pictures.</p>
<h3>2. Match with a line</h3><p>Pesticide — protects plants from pests and diseases; insecticide — used against insects; herbicide — used to destroy weeds.</p>
<h3>3. What may you do and what may you not?</h3><p>Look at the pictures and mark right (✓) or wrong (✗): holding a pesticide container; calling an adult; using an unlabelled container; reading the label.</p>
<h3>4. Why use them in moderation?</h3><p>Why must pesticides be used in moderation? They can pollute soil, water and air; useful insects and plants may also be harmed.</p>
<h3>5. Signs of poisoning</h3><p>Headache, nausea, dizziness, red or burning skin, difficulty breathing. <b>Your action:</b> I tell an adult immediately; I move away from the dangerous place; I go to a doctor; I don't take medicine on my own.</p>
<h3>6. Where is danger more likely?</h3><p>In which places may there be hazardous substances? Farm, place where cleaning products are stored, children's playground.</p>
<h3>7. Learn it!</h3><p>Pesticides are useful tools, but if we use them incorrectly they can harm human health.</p>
<h3>8. True or false?</h3><ol><li>Pesticides and insecticides are always harmless.</li><li>All chemical substances are useful.</li><li>Using pesticides without protective equipment is dangerous.</li><li>A substance may be used without reading the label.</li><li>Moderate use helps protect nature.</li></ol>
<h3>9. Life lesson</h3><p>The important thing is not to fear chemical substances but to know them well and use them safely.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Look at the warning signs on labels of hazardous substances at home and write their meaning.</li><li>Write three safety rules.</li><li>Explain why children must not use pesticides.</li></ol>`];

P[26] = [`<h2>Medicinal plants and natural remedies</h2>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><div class="dlg"><p>Asal caught a cold. Her grandmother made her a warm tea with mint and honey.</p><p>Jasur asked: — Why are some plants considered medicinal?</p><p>Teacher Kamol answered: — Because they contain useful chemical substances.</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3>
<p><b>Medicinal plant</b> — a plant used to prevent or treat certain diseases.</p>
<p><b>Natural remedy</b> — a remedy based on useful substances obtained from plants or other natural sources.</p>
<h3>Which plant is useful for what?</h3>
<table><tr><th>Plant</th><th>Benefit</th></tr><tr><td>Chamomile</td><td>Anti-inflammatory; helps with colds and coughs</td></tr><tr><td>Mint</td><td>Improves digestion, reduces nausea, soothes the throat</td></tr><tr><td>Aloe</td><td>Helps wounds heal faster, protects the skin, improves bowel function</td></tr><tr><td>Rose hip</td><td>Rich in vitamins, strengthens immunity, reduces tiredness</td></tr></table>
<div class="box y">Some medicines are made from medicinal plants. But natural remedies must also be used only on the advice of adults or a doctor, and in moderation!</div>`,
`<h3>1–2. Match</h3><p>Match each plant picture with its name (aloe, mint, chamomile, rose hip). Match the products in the pictures with the plants: infusion (tea), gel (ointment), vitamin drink.</p>
<h3>3. Chemistry detective</h3><p>Which pictures show medicinal plants? Tick (✓).</p>
<h3>4. What happens if medicinal plants disappear?</h3><p>Why must we protect them? They are an inseparable part of nature; they are important for many animals and insects; we must protect them so that future generations can also use them.</p>
<h3>5. Medical corner</h3><p>Medicinal plants can be useful, but they cannot always replace medicines prescribed by a doctor. Useful substances from plants are used in making medicines; they pass special tests before becoming a medicine. That is why making medicines from plants on your own is not recommended.</p>
<h3>6. Find the match!</h3><p>Match the plant with the part used: aloe — leaf; mint — leaf; rose hip — fruit; chamomile — flower.</p>
<h3>7. Think about it!</h3><p>Why is it not recommended to use medicinal plants in excess?</p>
<h3>8. Life lesson</h3><p>Nature has given us many medicinal plants. Protecting them and using them wisely protects both our health and nature.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 3 medicinal plants used in your home.</li><li>Find which part of each is used (leaf, flower or fruit).</li><li>Ask your parents or grandparents about one more medicinal plant and write it down.</li></ol>`];

P[27] = [`<h2>Pollutants and health</h2>
<p><b>Introduction:</b> Harmful substances that get into air, water and soil seriously damage human health. Recognising them and protecting ourselves is important for a healthy life!</p>
<h3>1. What is a pollutant?</h3><p>A <b>pollutant</b> is a substance or waste that gets into air, water or soil and can harm nature and human health.</p>
<h3>2. Main types of pollution</h3>
<table><tr><th>Air pollution</th><th>Water pollution</th><th>Soil pollution</th></tr><tr><td>Car exhaust; waste from factories and heating plants; dust and smoke</td><td>Sewage; household waste; oil and some chemicals</td><td>Plastic and household waste; incorrect use of pesticides; batteries and other hazardous waste</td></tr><tr><td><b>Effect:</b> can make breathing difficult and irritate the eyes and throat</td><td><b>Effect:</b> spoils drinking water quality, increases the risk of disease</td><td><b>Effect:</b> harms plants, animals and the food chain</td></tr></table>
<h3>3. How do pollutants enter the body?</h3><ul><li><b>Through breathing</b> — smoke, dust and harmful gases enter the body when we breathe.</li><li><b>Through water and food</b> — polluted water or food enters the body when we eat and drink.</li><li><b>Through the skin</b> — some harmful substances enter through contact with the skin.</li></ul>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> Invisible pollutants can also be harmful. A clean environment is an important condition for a healthy life.</div>`,
`<h3>1. Find the source of pollution!</h3><p>Sort the pictures into groups AIR / WATER / SOIL / PROTECTS NATURE: car exhaust, plastic waste, sorting waste, factory…</p>
<h3>2. Identify the route into the body!</h3><p>Match: through breathing, through the digestive system, through the skin.</p>
<h3>3. Make the city healthy!</h3><p>Suggest at least 4 changes to create a healthy environment in the first city (first city → healthy city).</p>
<h3>4. Ecologist observer</h3><p>In the picture, circle the pollution sources in RED and mark actions that protect nature in GREEN.</p>
<h3>5. True or false?</h3><ol><li>Dirty air harms only nature.</li><li>Drinking dirty water can cause disease.</li><li>Plastic waste pollutes the soil.</li><li>Planting trees improves air quality.</li><li>Pollutants are usually visible.</li></ol>
<h3>6. Why is it important?</h3><p>How does a dirty environment affect human health? Write briefly.</p>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Protecting the environment means protecting not only nature but also our own health.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 3 possible pollution sources around your home or school.</li><li>Suggest one solution for each.</li><li>Write 4–5 sentences on "My contribution to a clean environment".</li></ol>`];

P[28] = [`<h2>Plastics and ecology</h2>
<div class="dlg"><p><b>Kumush:</b> Teacher, does a plastic bottle lying on the ground harm nature?</p><p><b>Kumush:</b> Then why is it collected separately, not with ordinary rubbish?</p><p><b>Jasur:</b> Where are plastics collected?</p><p><b>Asal:</b> Can they be recycled?</p><p><b>Yoqut:</b> What happens if we don't do this?</p><p><b>Teacher Kamol:</b> Today we'll find answers to all of these!</p></div>
<h3>What is plastic?</h3><p><b>Plastic</b> is a light, strong artificial material widely used to make various items.</p>
<h3>Useful properties</h3><ul><li>Light</li><li>Waterproof</li><li>Strong</li><li>Many products are made from it</li></ul>
<h3>The ecological problem of plastic</h3><ul><li>It breaks down very slowly in nature.</li><li>It pollutes rivers and seas.</li><li>It can harm animals.</li></ul>
<h3>The 3R rule</h3><p><b>Reduce</b> — use less; <b>Reuse</b> — use again; <b>Recycle</b> — hand in for recycling.</p>
<h3>The life path of plastic</h3><p>Oil or natural gas → made in a factory → plastic raw material → plastic product → use → sorting → new product.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> Plastic itself is not harmful. Throwing it away incorrectly harms nature. Using it correctly and recycling it is everyone's job.</div>`,
`<h3>1. Which of these is plastic?</h3><p>Tick (✓) the plastic items in the pictures.</p>
<h3>2. Put in the right order</h3><p>Number the pictures 1 to 5 in the correct order.</p>
<h3>3. Find the hidden plastic waste</h3><p>Find the plastic waste hidden in the picture and mark which bin it should go into.</p>
<h3>4. Instead of single-use plastic bags</h3><p>What can be used instead of single-use plastic bags? Write 3 examples.</p>
<h3>5. Medical corner</h3><p>Why is burning plastic not recommended? When plastic burns, harmful gases are released. They damage the respiratory system. That is why plastic should not be burned but collected in the proper way and recycled.</p>
<h3>6. Chemistry corner — what is plastic made from?</h3><p>Oil or natural gas → chemical processing → plastic granules (raw material) → plastic items. Plastics are produced from oil or natural gas through various chemical processes.</p>
<h3>7. Green choice</h3><p>Which choice is better for nature? Tick (✓) the useful pictures.</p>
<h3>8. Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 5 plastic items in your home.</li><li>Find which of them can be recycled.</li><li>Write 3 suggestions with your family to reduce plastic waste.</li></ol>
<h3>9. Life lesson</h3><p>The future of every plastic item depends on your choice. Protecting nature starts with small habits.</p>`];

P[29] = [`<h2>Chemical waste</h2>
<p><i>Why can't these be thrown into an ordinary rubbish bin?</i> — HAZARDOUS CHEMICAL WASTE</p>
<h3>Chemical waste</h3><p><b>Chemical waste</b> is waste containing chemical substances that can harm the environment or human health.</p>
<h3>Examples of chemical waste</h3><p>Batteries, energy-saving lamps, used motor oil, paint and solvent residues, electronic waste.</p>
<h3>Why is it collected separately?</h3><p>Chemical waste → special safe collection → recycling or neutralisation → protecting nature.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> Chemical waste must not be mixed with ordinary rubbish!</div>
<h3>An interesting story</h3><p>Once, a small battery got wet and harmed the ground. That is why batteries must always be handed in at a special place.</p>
<h3>Think about it!</h3><p>If one battery harms nature, what will millions of batteries do?</p>`,
`<h3>1. Which is chemical waste?</h3><p>Tick (✓) the chemical waste.</p>
<h3>2. Choose the right container</h3><p>Connect the waste to the correct collection bin: battery, light bulb, motor oil container, paint residue, electronic device.</p>
<h3>3. The recycling path</h3><p>Put the pictures in the correct order: use → sorting waste → special collection → recycling → new product.</p>
<h3>4. Ecologist observer</h3><p>Find chemical waste thrown away incorrectly in the picture and mark it ✗. Mark safely collected ones ✓.</p>
<h3>5. Medical corner</h3><p>Children must not play with chemical waste. If you find such waste: don't touch it! Don't smell it! Tell an adult immediately!</p>
<h3>6. Chemistry corner — what is inside a battery?</h3><p>A battery contains metals and chemical substances. That is why it must be collected specially.</p>
<h3>7. Responsible citizen</h3><p>Which actions protect nature? Tick (✓) the correct answers.</p>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 3 examples of chemical waste found in your home.</li><li>Explain why they must not be thrown into ordinary rubbish.</li><li>Find out with your parents where batteries can be handed in in your neighbourhood.</li></ol>
<h3>9. Life lesson</h3><p>Collecting chemical waste correctly is part of protecting nature and human health.</p>`];
})(PAGES_TR.g6);
(function (P) {
P[30] = [`<h2>Soaps and shampoos</h2>
<p><i>Why doesn't oily dirt wash off with water alone, but comes off when soap is used?</i></p>
<h3>Key concepts</h3>
<p><b>Soap</b> — a cleaning product that helps separate dirt and grease from a surface with the help of water.</p>
<p><b>Shampoo</b> — a cleaning product designed to clean hair and the scalp.</p>
<h3>Interesting experiment: how does it work?</h3><p>Grease (dirt) + water only → the grease stays, it doesn't wash off completely. Grease + soap + water → soap separates the grease with the water → a clean surface.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> Soap does not destroy dirt — it turns it into a form that can be washed away with water.</div>
<h3>Why do we need them?</h3><ul><li>They clean dirt and grease.</li><li>They keep skin and hair clean.</li><li>They remove unpleasant smells.</li><li>They ensure hygiene and protect health.</li></ul>`,
`<h3>1. Which is for what?</h3><p>Match the items with their job: soap — cleans hands and body; shampoo — cleans hair; water — helps washing; towel — dries.</p>
<h3>2. The correct order</h3><p>Write the hand-washing steps in the correct order: wet your hands → apply soap → rub → rinse → dry.</p>
<h3>3. Where is the foam?</h3><p>Tick (✓) the situations where soap or shampoo is used.</p>
<h3>4. Medical corner</h3><p>Unwashed hands have many germs. Washing hands with soap helps reduce the spread of germs. Hands washed with soap are clean and safe.</p>
<h3>5. Chemistry corner — why does soap make foam?</h3><p>Water + foam → dirt separates from the surface. Foam wraps around the grease and carries it away from the surface together with the water.</p>
<h3>6. Clean habits</h3><p>Which of these habits are correct? Tick (✓): washing hands before eating; washing hands after coming in from outside; keeping hair clean; not sharing personal hygiene items with others.</p>
<h3>7. Think about it!</h3><p>Why doesn't oily dirt wash off completely with water alone?</p>
<h3>8. Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 3 kinds of cleaning products used in your home.</li><li>Write what each is used for.</li><li>Repeat the steps of washing hands correctly.</li></ol>
<h3>9. Life lesson</h3><p>Cleanliness is one of the simplest and most important habits for protecting health.</p>`];

P[31] = [`<h2>Paints and inks</h2>
<p>Today we get to know the chemical properties of paint and ink.</p>
<div class="dlg"><p><b>Teacher Saida (art):</b> Colours create emotion and beauty in art. Different paints suit different surfaces.</p><p><b>Po'lat:</b> Teacher, what is the difference between paint and ink?</p><p><b>Po'lat:</b> Why do some inks spread when water touches them, while others don't?</p><p><b>Teacher Kamol (chemistry)</b></p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3>
<p><b>Paint</b> — a substance used to give colour to an object or surface.</p>
<p><b>Ink</b> — a coloured liquid used for writing and printing.</p>
<h3>Where are colours used?</h3><p>Paint: house walls, cars, pictures. Ink: printers, pens, fabric.</p>
<h3>How is colour formed?</h3><p>A colouring substance (pigment or dye) is added to the paint or ink → the object looks coloured.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> Different paints and inks are used for different jobs.</div>
<p><b>Teacher Saida says:</b> colours create emotion and beauty in art; different paints suit different surfaces; choosing colours is the artist's skill.</p>
<p><b>Teacher Kamol says:</b> every paint contains a colouring substance (pigment); the composition of ink is made suitable for writing; because their compositions differ, their properties also differ.</p>`,
`<h3>1. Which is paint and which is ink?</h3><p>Place the pictures in the correct columns.</p>
<h3>2. Find the colour</h3><p>Tick (✓) which paint the given objects were painted with.</p>
<h3>3. Chemistry detective</h3><p>Find objects in the picture where paint or ink was used and tick them (✓).</p>
<h3>4. Find the match</h3><p>Match the objects with what is used to colour or write on them: wall — paint; pen — ink; printer — ink; picture (on paper) — paint.</p>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>Paints and inks must not be put in the mouth or touch the eyes. Wash your hands after using them.</p>
<h3>5. Who explained it?</h3><p>Mark who explained each piece of information (Teacher Saida / Teacher Kamol): colours are used in art; pigment gives paint its colour; ink is used for writing; choosing a brush is important.</p>
<h3>6. Write</h3><p>Write one similarity and one difference between paint and ink.</p>
<h3>7. Connect to life</h3><p>Write 5 objects at home or school where paint or ink is used, and where they are used.</p>
<h3>8. Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 3 examples of paint or ink used at home.</li><li>It is important to use paints and inks correctly and carefully.</li></ol>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Colours make our lives beautiful.</p>`];

P[32] = [`<h2>The chemistry of cooking</h2>
<div class="dlg"><p><b>Jasur:</b> Why does an egg go solid when it is heated?</p><p><b>Kumush:</b> Why does dough become bread after baking?</p><p><b>Teacher Kamol:</b> Because substances change under the effect of heat. Let's learn the chemical side of it.</p><p><b>Malika opa (school cook)</b></p></div>
<h3>Cooking chemistry</h3><p><b>Cooking chemistry</b> — processes in cooking linked to changes in the composition and properties of substances under the effect of heat.</p>
<h3>What happens during cooking?</h3><p>Egg — sets solid; dough — turns into bread; meat — cooks; rice — softens.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> Not every change is chemical. But many processes in cooking are linked to the appearance of new properties.</div>
<h3>The role of heat</h3><p>Raw product → heating → cooked product.</p>
<h3>Chemistry corner: why does it change?</h3><p>The substances in the raw product change under the effect of heat. The composition of substances changes and new properties appear.</p>`,
`<h3>1. Before and after cooking</h3><p>Match the pictures.</p>
<h3>2. What does heat do?</h3><p>Match: melts; boils; cooks.</p>
<h3>3. Cook's observation</h3><p>Tick (✓) the processes in the picture in which a chemical change occurred.</p>
<h3>4. Who said it?</h3><p>Mark who said each idea (Malika opa — cook / Teacher Kamol — chemistry): cooking food correctly is important; heat changes substances; the volume of dough increases; chemistry studies these changes.</p>
<h3>5. Medical corner</h3><p>Cooking food enough helps reduce harmful microorganisms in food. It is also important not to burn the food.</p>
<h3>6. Chemistry corner</h3><p>Why does the smell of bread change? Dough → baking → new substances with a pleasant smell form.</p>
<h3>7. Connect to life</h3><p>Write 5 situations at home or school in which chemical changes occur during cooking.</p>
<h3>8. Homework</h3><ol><li>Write 3 dishes cooked at home.</li><li>Write what changes happen when they are prepared.</li><li>Write 3 sentences about the role of heat in cooking.</li></ol>`];

P[33] = [`<h2>Sport and chemistry</h2>
<div class="dlg"><p><b>Teacher Farangiz (physical education):</b> Why do we breathe fast after running? Why do we want to drink a lot of water?</p><p><b>Teacher Kamol (chemistry):</b> Because oxygen and water play an important role in producing energy in our body.</p></div>
<h3>Key concept: sport and chemistry</h3><p>During sport, chemical processes take place in the body, such as producing energy, using oxygen and exchanging water.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> Drinking water and eating correctly during sport are important for the body.</div>
<h3>Where does energy come from?</h3><p>Food → digestion → energy is produced → movement.</p>
<h3>Why do we breathe fast?</h3><p>Oxygen is taken in by breathing → it helps produce energy in the body → gives strength for movement.</p>
<h3>Why do we drink water?</h3><p>During sport we sweat → the body loses water → drinking water refills the lost fluid.</p>
<p>Muscles work → energy is produced → breathing speeds up and more oxygen takes part → sweating occurs → we need to drink water.</p>`,
`<h3>1. The athlete's body</h3><p>Match: running — leads to fast breathing; drinking water — refills lost fluid; eating food — is the source of energy.</p>
<h3>2. Who uses more energy?</h3><p>In which sports is more energy used: running, cycling, swimming, jumping? Tick (✓) and write why you think so.</p>
<h3>3. Chemistry observation</h3><p>Find and tick (✓) the processes linked with chemistry in the picture: drinking water, breathing, sweating, muscles working, heat being produced.</p>
<h3>4. Who explained it?</h3><p>Mark who explained the information (Teacher Farangiz / Teacher Kamol): warming up is important; you must drink water after exercise; oxygen takes part in producing energy; sweating helps cool the body.</p>
<h3>5. Coach's advice</h3><ul><li>Warm up before sport.</li><li>Drink water during exercise.</li><li>Eat correctly.</li><li>Rest after exercise.</li></ul>
<h3>Medical corner</h3><p>Warming up before exercise prevents muscle injuries. Resting after exercise helps the body recover. Drinking enough water keeps us healthy.</p>
<h3>6. Chemistry corner — why does sweat form?</h3><p>We move → the body heats up → sweating occurs → the body cools down.</p>
<h3>7. Connect to life</h3><p>Write 5 things you notice after doing sport at home or school.</p>
<h3>8. Homework</h3><ol><li>Write the sport you like.</li><li>Write what changes happen in the body during sport.</li><li>Explain why you must drink water after exercise.</li></ol>`];

P[34] = [`<h2>The dream of becoming a chemist or a doctor</h2>
<div class="dlg"><p><b>Asal:</b> Teacher, are the jobs of a chemist and a doctor connected?</p><p><b>Teacher Kamol:</b> Yes. Chemistry is very important in creating new medicines and in protecting people's health.</p><p><b>Asal:</b> If we know chemistry well, which professions can we take up?</p></div>
<h3>Key concepts</h3>
<p><b>Chemist</b> — studies substances and creates new materials and useful products.</p>
<p><b>Doctor</b> — protects human health, diagnoses and treats diseases.</p>
<h3>Where is chemistry needed?</h3><p>Laboratory, medicines, hospital, agriculture, food, industry, ecology, cosmetics.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Remember!</b> Chemistry is the foundation of many professions.</div>
<h3>What did we learn during the year?</h3><p>What is chemistry? Substance and body; composition of substances; atom and element; oxygen and carbon dioxide; states of matter; melting and boiling temperatures; mixtures and solutions; chemical reactions; acids and alkalis; composition of water… Future — my profession!</p>`,
`<h3>1. Find the profession</h3><p>Match the professions with their job: chemist — studies new substances; doctor — treats patients; pharmacist — works with medicines.</p>
<h3>2. Who will I become?</h3><p>These qualities are very important in these professions. Tick (✓) the suitable qualities for each (chemist / doctor): observant, responsible, curious, honest, patient.</p>
<h3>3. Chemistry in my life</h3><p>Find the places in the pictures where chemistry is used and tick them (✓).</p>
<h3>A life story</h3><p>Once there was an ordinary schoolboy who became interested in chemistry. He read many books, did experiments and worked towards his dream. Years passed; he became a scientist and, together with others, took part in creating a new medicine. Today he helps thousands of people. If you work hard now, your dreams will come true!</p>
<h3>My future</h3><p>In the future I dream of becoming ______ because ______.</p>
<h3>Professions corner — professions linked with chemistry</h3><table><tr><td><b>Chemist</b> — creates new substances, materials and products.</td><td><b>Doctor</b> — treats people and protects their health.</td><td><b>Pharmacist</b> — prepares, stores and distributes medicines.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Lab technician</b> — carries out analyses in the laboratory and helps with experiments.</td><td><b>Agronomist</b> — studies plant growth and increases yields.</td><td><b>Cosmetologist</b> — cares for skin and works with cosmetic products.</td></tr></table>
<h3>Homework</h3><ol><li>Write the profession that interests you.</li><li>Write how chemistry helps in this profession.</li><li>Write what you should learn from today to achieve your dream.</li></ol>
<h3>Life lesson</h3><p>Big dreams start with small steps. The chemistry you are learning today may become the basis of a profession that benefits people tomorrow. <b>KNOWLEDGE · EXPERIENCE · UPBRINGING</b></p>`];
})(PAGES_TR.g6);
(function (P) {
P[0] = [`<h2>Kimyoni o'rganishni boshlaymiz — 6-sinf kimyosi</h2>
<p><b>Asosiy tushunchalar diagnostikasi.</b> <b>Asosiy savol:</b> Biz kimyodan qancha narsani o'rganib bo'ldik va 7-sinfga qanchalik tayyormiz?</p>
<h3>6-sinfda o'rgangan asosiy tushunchalar</h3>
<table><tr><th>Modda</th><th>Atom</th><th>Molekula</th></tr><tr><td>Ma'lum tarkib va xossalarga ega bo'lgan narsa.</td><td>Musbat zaryadli yadro va manfiy zaryadli elektronlardan iborat zarracha.</td><td>Moddaning tarkibi va xossalarini ifodalovchi atomlar birikmasi.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Ion</th><th>Aralashma</th><th>Kimyoviy reaksiya</th></tr><tr><td>Elektron qabul qilish yoki berish natijasida hosil bo'lgan zaryadli zarracha.</td><td>Ikki yoki undan ortiq moddaning fizik aralashmasi.</td><td>Bir yoki bir nechta moddadan yangi moddalar hosil bo'ladigan jarayon.</td></tr></table>
<div class="box">6-sinfda biz moddalarni o'rgandik. 7-sinfda ularning ichki qonuniyatlarini o'rganamiz.</div>`,
`<p>Savollarga javob bering va natijalaringizni baholang.</p>
<h3>1. Tushunchani aniqlang</h3><p>Berilgan misollarni to'g'ri guruhlarga ajrating (JISM / MODDA / ATOM / MOLEKULA / ION): temir mix, H₂O (bitta molekula), Na⁺ ioni, xlor atomi, shakar, shisha stakan.</p>
<h3>2. Xossani aniqlang</h3><p>Quyidagilar fizik yoki kimyoviy xossa ekanini aniqlang: 1. Suv rangsiz. 2. Temir zanglaydi. 3. Shakar suvda eriydi. 4. Yog' yonadi.</p>
<h3>3. Zarrachalarni farqlang</h3><p>Uchta zarracha modeli berilgan. Har birining nomi va bitta xossasini yozing (atom, molekula, ion).</p>
<h3>4. Aralashmani ajrating</h3><p>Quyidagi aralashmalarni ajratish uchun qaysi usul mos? 1. Qum + suv. 2. Tuz + suv. 3. Temir qipiqlari + qum.</p>
<h3>5. Reaksiyani aniqlang</h3><p>Qaysi holatlarda yangi modda hosil bo'ladi? Belgilang (✓): muzning erishi, qog'ozning yonishi, suvning qaynashi, temirning zanglashi.</p>
<h3>6. Mening boshlang'ich natijam</h3><p>O'zingizni baholang: <b>Tushunaman</b> — 6-sinf mavzularini yaxshi tushunaman, 7-sinfga tayyorman! <b>Ishonchim komil emas</b> — ba'zi mavzularni takrorlashim kerak. <b>Yordam kerak</b> — o'qituvchim yordamiga muhtojman, qiyinchiliklarim bor.</p>
<h3>Uyga vazifa</h3><p>Daftaringizda "Mening kimyo xaritam"ni tuzing: (1) 6-sinfdan eslab qolgan 3 ta asosiy tushuncha; (2) eslab qolgan 2 ta tajriba yoki hodisa; (3) 6-sinfdagi eng qiziqarli 1 ta mavzu; (4) 7-sinfda kimyodan nimani o'rganishni xohlaysiz?</p>`];

P[1] = [`<h2>Kimyoning rivojlanishi va zamonaviy kimyo</h2>
<p>Kimyo moddalarni: ularning tarkibi, tuzilishi, xossalari va ularda sodir bo'ladigan o'zgarishlarni o'rganadi.</p>
<p><b>Asosiy savol:</b> Kimyo fani qanday paydo bo'lgan va bugungi kungacha qanday rivojlangan?</p>
<h3>Kimyoning tarixiy rivojlanishidagi asosiy bosqichlar</h3>
<table><tr><th>Qadimgi davr</th><th>O'rta asrlar</th><th>XVII–XVIII asrlar</th><th>XIX asr</th><th>XX–XXI asrlar</th></tr><tr><td>Odamlar tabiatdagi moddalarni kuzatish va amaliy tajriba orqali o'rganishgan.</td><td>Alkimyogarlar moddalarni o'zgartirishga urinishgan va "falsafa toshi" kabi sirli moddalarni izlashgan.</td><td>Moddalarni o'rganishda ilmiy yondashuv shakllandi. Kimyoviy hodisalarni tajriba, o'lchash va miqdoriy o'rganish rivojlana boshladi.</td><td>Kimyoviy elementlar kashf etildi, davriy jadval yaratildi va kimyoviy nazariyalar asoslandi.</td><td>Zamonaviy kimyo moddalarni molekulyar darajada o'rganadi va yangi xossali materiallar yaratadi.</td></tr></table>
<h3>Kundalik hayotimizda zamonaviy kimyoning muhim sohalari</h3>
<ul><li><b>Tibbiyot va farmatsevtika</b> — kimyo kasalliklarni davolash uchun dorilar, vaksinalar va diagnostika vositalarini yaratishda asosiy rol o'ynaydi.</li><li><b>Yangi materiallar</b> — turli maqsadlar uchun mustahkam, yengil, issiqlikka chidamli materiallar ishlab chiqariladi.</li><li><b>Energetika</b> — yoqilg'ilar, batareyalar, quyosh panellari va vodorod energiyasi manbalari; kelajakda kimyoning roli katta.</li><li><b>Ekologiya va atrof-muhit</b> — kimyo iflos suvni tozalash, chiqindilarni qayta ishlash va atrof-muhit ifloslanishini kamaytirish yechimlarini taklif qiladi.</li></ul>`,
`<h3>1. Tarixiy bosqichlarni tartiblang</h3><p>Raqamlarni 1 dan 5 gacha to'g'ri tartibda yozing: ilmiy yondashuv shakllandi va kimyo fani rivojlana boshladi; kimyoviy elementlar kashf etildi va davriy jadval yaratildi; zamonaviy kimyo moddalarni molekulyar darajada o'rganib, yangi materiallar yaratadi; alkimyo davrida moddalarni o'zgartirish va sirli moddalarni izlashga urinishlar bo'ldi; odamlar tabiatdagi moddalar bilan amaliy tajribalar o'tkazishgan.</p>
<h3>2. Zamonaviy kimyoning qo'llanilishini moslang</h3><p>1. Dori-darmonlar ishlab chiqarish — 2. Yangi materiallar ishlab chiqarish — 3. Iflos suvni tozalash va chiqindilarni qayta ishlash — 4. Yoqilg'i va energiya olish. A. Ekologiya B. Energetika C. Materiallar D. Tibbiyot.</p>
<h3>3. O'ylang va yozing</h3><p>Sizningcha, kelajakda kimyo fani qanday rivojlanadi? Fikringizni 2–3 gapda yozing.</p>
<h3>4. O'tmish, bugun va kelajak</h3><p>Har bir holat qaysi davrga tegishli ekanini aniqlang va ✓ bilan belgilang: 1. Bronza, oltin yoki kumush olish. 2. Kislorod elementi kashf etildi. 3. Odamlar o'simlik va minerallarni dori sifatida ishlatishgan. 4. Plastmassa va polimerlar yaratildi. 5. Zamonaviy dori-darmonlar ishlab chiqariladi.</p>
<h3>5. Jarayonni tahlil qiling</h3><p>Quyidagi jarayonning har bir bosqichi kimyoning qaysi sohasida qo'llaniladi? Tadqiqot → tajriba va kuzatish → ilmiy tushuntirish → zamonaviy texnologiya.</p>
<h3>6. Bugun nimani o'rgandim?</h3><p>Gaplarni to'ldiring: Kimyo tarixi … ; Zamonaviy kimyoning hayotimizdagi roli … ; Kelajakdagi kasbimda kimyo … .</p>
<h3>Uyga vazifa</h3><ol><li>Kimyoning rivojlanish bosqichlarini qisqacha tushuntiring.</li><li>Atrofingizdagi kimyo yordamida yaratilgan 3 ta buyum yoki mahsulotni yozing va ularning kimyo bilan qanday bog'liqligini tushuntiring.</li></ol>`];

P[2] = [`<h2>Sharq olimlari va kimyoning rivojlanishi</h2>
<p>Sharq olimlari kimyo fanining rivojlanishiga katta hissa qo'shgan.</p>
<p><b>Asosiy savol:</b> Sharq olimlari kimyoning rivojlanishiga qanday hissa qo'shgan?</p>
<h3>Sharq olimlarining kimyoga qo'shgan hissasi</h3>
<table><tr><th>Abu Rayhon Beruniy</th><th>Ibn Sino</th><th>Jobir ibn Hayyon</th><th>Al-Xorazmiy</th></tr><tr><td>Moddalarning tarkibi va xossalarini o'rgangan, tahlil usullarini tasvirlagan. Minerallarni o'rganib, ularning xossalarini aniqlagan. Kuzatish, o'lchash va aniq hisob-kitobga katta hissa qo'shgan.</td><td>Dorivor moddalar va ularning inson organizmiga ta'sirini o'rgangan. Haydash (distillyatsiya) usulini batafsil tasvirlagan. Kimyo va tibbiyot o'rtasidagi bog'liqlikni tizimlashtirgan.</td><td>Moddalar bilan ko'plab tajribalar o'tkazgan. Laboratoriya jihozlari (shisha idishlar), suyuqliklarni tayyorlash va kristallash usullarini tasvirlagan. Tajribalar orqali kislotalar, spirtlar va tuzlar olgan.</td><td>Laboratoriya amaliyotida o'lchash va hisoblash usullarini rivojlantirgan. Sof moddalarni tasniflash va ularning xossalarini o'rganishga katta hissa qo'shgan.</td></tr></table>
<h3>Sharq olimlarining ilmiy yondashuvi</h3>
<ol><li><b>Kuzatish</b> — tabiat hodisalari va moddalarni diqqat bilan kuzatish.</li><li><b>Tajriba</b> — moddalarni sinab ko'rish, ularning xossalari va o'zgarishlarini o'rganish.</li><li><b>O'lchash</b> — miqdorlarni aniq o'lchash va natijalarni tahlil qilish.</li><li><b>Qayd etish</b> — kuzatishlarni tartibli shaklda yozib borish.</li><li><b>Xulosa chiqarish</b> — natijalar asosida xulosa chiqarish va yangi bilim yaratish.</li></ol>`,
`<h3>1. Qadimgi laboratoriya jihozlari</h3><p>Moslang: 1. Analitik tarozi; 2. O'lchov kolbasi; 3. Haydash apparati; 4. Chinni hovoncha — A. Alembik (haydash idishi) B. Hovoncha va dasta C. Tarozi D. Shisha kolba.</p>
<h3>2. Kim nima qilgan?</h3><p>Har bir olimni uning kimyoga qo'shgan hissasi bilan moslang: A. Abu Rayhon Beruniy B. Ibn Sino C. Jobir ibn Hayyon D. Al-Xorazmiy — 1. Haydash usulini tasvirlagan va dorivor moddalar haqida yozgan. 2. Moddalar bilan ko'p tajribalar o'tkazgan, laboratoriya jihozlarini rivojlantirgan. 3. Zichlikni aniqlash usullarini o'rgangan va minerallarni tasvirlagan. 4. Moddalarni tasniflash va o'lchashga katta hissa qo'shgan.</p>
<h3>3. Vaqt chizig'i</h3><p>Olimlarni eng qadimgisidan eng keyingisigacha tartiblang: Abu Rayhon Beruniy (973–1048), Ibn Sino (980–1037), Al-Xorazmiy (IX asr), Jobir ibn Hayyon (VIII–IX asr).</p>
<h3>4. Ilmiy usulni aniqlang</h3><p>Quyidagi faoliyatlarda qaysi ilmiy usul qo'llanilgan (kuzatish, tajriba, o'lchash, qayd etish, xulosa chiqarish)? 1. Beruniy turli metall va minerallarni to'plab, zichligini aniqlagan. 2. Ibn Sino o'simliklardan dorivor modda olgan va haydash usulini tasvirlagan. 3. Jobir moddalarni aralashtirgan, rang o'zgarishini sinagan va xossalarini o'rgangan. 4. Al-Xorazmiy moddalarni o'lchab, miqdorini hisoblagan. 5. Olimlar tajribalari haqida kitoblar yozib, bilimlarini kelajak avlodlarga yetkazgan.</p>
<h3>5. Kecha va bugun — laboratoriya</h3><p>Qadimgi jihozni uning zamonaviy muqobili bilan moslang.</p>
<h3>6. Tahliliy savol</h3><p>Nima uchun Sharq olimlari moddalar ustida shuncha ko'p tajriba, o'lchash va kuzatish o'tkazgan? Sizningcha, bu kimyoning rivojlanishi uchun qanday ahamiyatga ega bo'lgan?</p>
<h3>Bugun nimani o'rgandim?</h3><p>Gaplarni to'ldiring: Sharq olimlarining kimyoga eng katta hissasi … edi. Ilmiy usulning asosiy bosqichlari … . Menga eng qiziq bo'lgan olim … , chunki … .</p>`];

P[3] = [`<h2>Laboratoriyada xavfsizlik va aniqlik</h2>
<p>Kimyo laboratoriyasida doimo muhim qoidalarga amal qilishimiz kerak. Xavfsiz xatti-harakat, aniq ish va diqqatli kuzatish ishonchli natijalarga olib keladi.</p>
<p><b>Asosiy savol:</b> Nima uchun laboratoriyada xavfsizlik ham, aniqlik ham muhim?</p>
<h3>Laboratoriyada ishlashning asosiy qoidalari</h3>
<table><tr><th>Xavfsizlik</th><th>Tartib va tozalik</th><th>Ehtiyotkorlik</th><th>Aniqlik</th><th>Muammo bo'lsa</th></tr><tr><td>Himoya vositalaridan foydalaning (ko'zoynak, xalat, qo'lqop).</td><td>Ish joyingizni toza va tartibli saqlang; keraksiz narsalarni olib qo'ying.</td><td>Kimyoviy moddalarga tegmang va ularni hidlamang. Faqat o'qituvchi ko'rsatmasi bo'yicha ishlang.</td><td>Ko'rsatmani diqqat bilan o'qing. Aniq va kerakli miqdorni oling. Shkalani ko'z sathida to'g'ri o'qing. Natijalarni daftarga tartibli yozing.</td><td>Har qanday g'ayrioddiy holat, to'kilish yoki baxtsiz hodisa haqida darhol o'qituvchiga xabar bering.</td></tr></table>
<div class="box y"><b>XAVFSIZLIK + ANIQLIK + TARTIB = ISHONCHLI TAJRIBA NATIJASI!</b></div>`,
`<h3>1. To'g'ri yoki noto'g'ri</h3><ol><li>Laboratoriyada himoya ko'zoynagi taqish shart emas.</li><li>Kimyoviy moddalar bilan ehtiyotkorlik va to'g'ri muomala qilish kerak.</li><li>Natijalar daftarga aniq yozilishi kerak.</li><li>Ish joyi doimo toza va tartibli saqlanishi kerak.</li><li>Tajribalarni o'qituvchi nazoratisiz o'tkazish mumkin.</li><li>Modda to'kilsa, darhol o'qituvchiga xabar berish kerak.</li></ol>
<h3>2. Vaziyatni tahlil qiling</h3><p>Quyidagi holatlarda qaysi qoida buzilgan? 1. O'quvchi moddani hidlayapti — A. Moddani to'g'ridan-to'g'ri hidlamang. B. Yorug'lik manbasidan uzoq turing va yorliqni o'qing. C. Derazani oching, moddani ushlamang. 2. Stolga kislota to'kildi — nima qilish kerak? A. Hech narsa. B. Artmasdan ishni davom ettirish. C. O'qituvchiga xabar berish, ko'p suv bilan yuvish va ish joyini tozalash. 3. Ish joyi tartibsiz — A. Muammo yo'q. B. Tozalab, hammasini tartibga keltirish. C. Shundayligicha qoldirish.</p>
<h3>3. O'ylang va yozing</h3><p>Nima uchun laboratoriya xavfsizlik qoidalari muhim?</p>
<h3>4. Bo'sh joylarni to'ldiring</h3><p>So'zlar: toza, aniq, xavfsizlik, o'qituvchi, kuzatish, tartib, kimyoviy moddalar, maxsus jihozlar. Laboratoriyada doimo … foydalanishimiz kerak. Ruxsatsiz … tegmasligimiz kerak. … toza saqlanishi kerak. Moddalarning holati va o'zgarishlarini … kerak. Ish joyimiz … va tartibli bo'lishi kerak. Tajriba tugagach, ish joyini … kerak. Laboratoriyada ovqat yemaymiz va ichmaymiz. Muammo bo'lsa, darhol … xabar berishimiz kerak.</p>
<h3>5. Belgilarni moslang</h3><p>A. Yonuvchan modda; B. Zaharli modda; C. O'yuvchi (korroziv) modda; D. Zararli yoki qitiqlovchi modda (nafas yo'llarini qitiqlaydi); E. Atrof-muhit uchun xavfli modda; F. Siqilgan gaz.</p>
<h3>6. Tahlil</h3><p>Aniqlik tajriba natijasiga qanday ta'sir qiladi? O'z misollaringiz bilan tushuntiring.</p>
<h3>Uyga vazifa</h3><ol><li>Jadvalni to'ldiring: jihoz nomi, u nima uchun ishlatiladi, qaysi xavfsizlik qoidasi bilan bog'liq.</li><li>Laboratoriyada xavfsiz va aniq ishlash uchun 5 ta qoida yozing.</li></ol>
<h3>Bugun nimani o'rgandim?</h3><p>Laboratoriyadagi eng muhim qoida … . Xavfsiz tajriba … . Aniqlik … bilan bog'liq. Qoidalarga amal qilsak, … mumkin.</p>`];

P[4] = [`<h2>Laboratoriya jihozlari: 7-sinfdagi amaliy ko'nikmalar</h2>
<p>Har bir laboratoriya jihozining o'z vazifasi bor. Jihozni to'g'ri tanlash va ishlatish aniq va xavfsiz tajriba o'tkazishning muhim shartidir.</p>
<p><b>Asosiy savol:</b> Har bir jihoz qaysi holatlarda ishlatiladi va uni xavfsiz ishlatish nima uchun muhim?</p>
<h3>Ushlash / aralashtirish</h3><ul><li><b>1. Probirka</b> — oz miqdordagi moddalarni aralashtirish, qizdirish va kimyoviy reaksiyalar o'tkazish uchun.</li><li><b>2. Stakan (beaker)</b> — suyuqliklarni aralashtirish, qizdirish va saqlash uchun; hajmni taxminan o'lchaydi.</li><li><b>3. Konussimon kolba (Erlenmeyer kolbasi)</b> — suyuqliklarni aralashtirish, reaksiyalar o'tkazish va chayqatish uchun qulay.</li></ul>
<h3>O'lchash</h3><ul><li><b>4. O'lchov silindri</b> — suyuqliklar hajmini aniq o'lchash uchun.</li><li><b>5. Tomizgich (pipetka)</b> — oz miqdordagi suyuqlikni aniq o'lchash va tomizish uchun.</li></ul>
<h3>Ajratish / qizdirish</h3><ul><li><b>6. Voronka</b> — suyuqliklarni quyish va filtrlash uchun.</li><li><b>7. Probirka ushlagich</b> — qizdirish yoki tajriba vaqtida probirkani ushlash uchun.</li><li><b>8. Spirt lampasi</b> — moddalarni qizdirish uchun issiqlik manbai.</li></ul>
<h3>Tayanch</h3><ul><li><b>9. Shtativ va qisqich</b> — tajriba va qizdirish vaqtida jihozlarni vertikal holatda ushlab turish uchun.</li><li><b>10. Chinni (keramik) hovoncha</b> — qattiq moddalarni maydalash, aralashtirish va kukun tayyorlash uchun.</li></ul>
<h3>Laboratoriya jihozini tanlash bosqichlari</h3><ol><li><b>Vazifani aniqlang</b> — qanday natija olmoqchisiz?</li><li><b>Kerakli jarayonni aniqlang</b> — qanday jarayon kerak (aralashtirish, qizdirish yoki o'lchash)?</li><li><b>Mos jihozni tanlang</b> — har bir vazifa uchun qaysi jihoz mos?</li><li><b>To'g'ri va xavfsiz ishlating</b> — xavfsizlik qoidalariga amal qilib, tajribani o'tkazing.</li></ol>
<div class="box y">JIHOZ NOMINI BILISH YETARLI EMAS — UNING VAZIFASINI TUSHUNISH VA AMALDA TO'G'RI ISHLATISH KERAK!</div>`,
`<h3>1. To'g'ri yoki noto'g'ri</h3><ol><li>O'lchov silindri suyuqliklar hajmini o'lchash uchun ishlatiladi.</li><li>Suyuqlik hajmini stakandagi belgilar yordamida aniq o'lchash mumkin.</li><li>Pipetka oz miqdordagi suyuqlikni aniq o'lchash uchun ishlatiladi.</li><li>Voronka faqat suyuqlik quyish uchun ishlatiladi.</li><li>Probirka ushlagich qizdirish vaqtida probirkani ushlash uchun ishlatiladi.</li><li>Shtativ va qisqich jihozlarni ushlash va tajriba qurilmasini yig'ish uchun ishlatiladi.</li></ol>
<h3>2. Rasmlarga qarab nomlarini yozing</h3>
<h3>3. Moslashtirish</h3><p>Probirka, stakan, o'lchov silindri, pipetka, voronka, spirt lampasi, shtativ va qisqich, konussimon kolba, chinni hovoncha, probirka ushlagich — vazifalari bilan moslang.</p>
<h3>4. To'g'ri jihozni tanlang</h3><ol><li>10 mL eritmani o'lchash — pipetka / stakan / o'lchov silindri.</li><li>30 mL eritmani aralashtirish — stakan / pipetka / konussimon kolba.</li><li>Qattiq modda va suyuqlik aralashmasini filtrlash — voronka / pipetka / stakan.</li><li>Probirkadagi moddani qizdirish — probirka ushlagich / stakan / shtativ.</li><li>Qattiq moddani kukunga aylantirish — chinni hovoncha / spirt lampasi / stakan.</li></ol>
<h3>5. Vaziyatni tahlil qiling</h3><p>1. 100 mL eritmani boshqa kolbaga quyish — A. Pipetka B. Voronka C. Stakan. 2. 25 mL suvni aniq o'lchash — A. Stakan B. O'lchov silindri C. Probirka. 3. Probirkadagi moddani qizdirish — A. Probirka ushlagich B. Konussimon kolba C. Spirt lampasi.</p>
<h3>6. O'ylang va yozing</h3><p>O'lchov silindridan foydalanganda nima uchun hajmni ko'z sathida o'qish kerak?</p>
<h3>Uyga vazifa</h3><p>Jadvalni to'ldiring: jihoz nomi; vazifasi (nima uchun ishlatiladi?); qaysi holatda ishlatiladi?</p>
<h3>Bugun nimani o'rgandim?</h3><p>Kimyo laboratoriyasida har bir jihozning o'z … bor. Jihoz tanlashda eng muhim omil … . Jihozlarni beparvo ishlatmaymiz, chunki u … bo'lishi mumkin. Tajribadagi aniqlik … natija olishga yordam beradi.</p>`];

P[5] = [`<h2>O'lchash: massa, hajm va harorat</h2>
<p>Kimyoda tajribalarda aniq natija olish uchun massa, hajm va haroratni o'lchaymiz. To'g'ri jihozni tanlash va uni to'g'ri ishlatish juda muhim.</p>
<p><b>Asosiy savol:</b> Nima uchun kimyo tajribalarida massa, hajm va harorat muhim?</p>
<h3>Kattaliklar va laboratoriya jihozlari</h3>
<table><tr><th>MASSA — moddadagi materiya miqdori</th><th>HAJM — modda egallagan joy</th><th>HARORAT — moddaning qanchalik issiq yoki sovuqligi o'lchovi</th></tr><tr><td>Grammda (g) o'lchanadi. Jihoz: elektron tarozi.</td><td>Millilitr (mL) yoki litrda (L) o'lchanadi. Jihoz: o'lchov silindri, pipetka, stakan.</td><td>Selsiy gradusida (°C) o'lchanadi. Jihoz: termometr.</td></tr>
<tr><td>• Tarozini tekis yuzaga qo'ying.<br>• Tarozini nolga keltiring.<br>• Moddani idishga soling.<br>• Moddaga qo'l tekkizmang.<br>• Natijani to'g'ri birlik (g) bilan yozing.</td><td>• Silindrni vertikal tuting.<br>• Hajmni ko'z sathida o'qing.<br>• Kichik hajmlar uchun pipetkadan foydalaning.<br>• Aniq hajmni aniqlang.<br>• Natijani to'g'ri birlik (mL yoki L) bilan yozing.</td><td>• Termometrni suyuqlikka botiring.<br>• Idish tubi yoki devoriga tegizmang.<br>• Ko'rsatkich barqarorlashishini kuting.<br>• Shkalani ko'z sathida o'qing.<br>• Natijani °C da yozing.</td></tr></table>
<h3>O'lchash aniqligiga ta'sir qiluvchi omillar</h3>
<ol><li><b>Meniskni to'g'ri o'qish (hajm)</b> — ko'z sathida, meniskning pastki qismidan o'qing. Yuqoridan o'qish kattaroq natija beradi.</li><li><b>Silindrni vertikal tutish</b> — silindr qiya bo'lsa, natija noto'g'ri bo'ladi.</li><li><b>Tarozidan to'g'ri foydalanish (massa)</b> — idishni qo'ying, nolga keltiring, so'ng moddani qo'shing. Nolga keltirilmasa, natija noto'g'ri bo'ladi.</li></ol>
<div class="box y"><b>Eslab qoling!</b> Aniq o'lchash ishonchli tajriba natijalarining asosidir. Doimo to'g'ri jihozdan foydalaning va qoidalarga amal qiling!</div>`,
`<h3>1. To'g'ri yoki noto'g'ri</h3><ol><li>Massa grammda (g) o'lchanadi.</li><li>O'lchov silindridagi suyuqlik meniski yuqori qismidan o'qilishi kerak.</li><li>Haroratni o'lchashda termometr suyuqlikka qisman botiriladi.</li><li>Pipetka oz miqdordagi suyuqlikni aniq o'lchash uchun ishlatiladi.</li><li>Moddani qo'ygandan so'ng tarozini qimirlatmaslik kerak.</li><li>O'lchash natijalari doimo birlik bilan yoziladi (g, mL, °C).</li></ol>
<h3>2. Har bir vaziyatni mos jihoz bilan moslang</h3><p>1. 150 mL suvni o'lchash. 2. 2,5 g tuzni tortish. 3. 3 mL kislota eritmasini o'lchash. 4. Eritma haroratini o'lchash (45 °C). 5. 25 mL eritmani boshqa idishga quyish. 6. To'g'ridan-to'g'ri tortib bo'lmaydigan modda massasini o'lchash (idishdan foydalanib). — A. Elektron tarozi B. O'lchov silindri C. Pipetka D. Termometr E. Voronka F. Idish + tarozi.</p>
<h3>3. Rasmlarga qarab natijalarni yozing</h3><p>Natija: … mL; … g; … °C.</p>
<h3>4. Vaziyatni tahlil qiling</h3><p>Quyidagi xatolar tajribaga qanday ta'sir qiladi va ularni qanday tuzatish mumkin? 1. Meniskni yuqori qismidan o'qish. 2. Silindrni qiya holda o'lchash. 3. Tortishdan oldin tarozini nolga keltirmaslik. 4. Pipetkani gorizontal ushlash. 5. Termometrni idish tubiga tekkizish.</p>
<h3>5. To'g'ri tartibda joylashtiring</h3><p>O'lchov silindri bilan 25 mL eritmani o'lchash bosqichlarini tartiblang (1–5): silindrni tekis yuzaga qo'yish; eritmaning bir qismini silindrga quyish; meniskni 25 mL belgisiga keltirish; hajmni ko'z sathida o'qish; natijani 25 mL deb yozish.</p>
<h3>6. O'ylang va yozing</h3><p>Nima uchun kimyo tajribalarida hajmni aniq o'lchash juda muhim?</p>
<h3>Uyga vazifa</h3><ol><li>Jadvalni to'ldiring: kattalik, birlik, jihoz (massa, hajm, harorat).</li><li>Quyidagilarni o'lchash uchun qaysi jihozdan foydalanasiz: 3 g tuz; 80 °C li eritma harorati?</li></ol>
<h3>Bugun nimani o'rgandim?</h3><p>Massa … (g) da … yordamida o'lchanadi. Hajm … (mL) da … yordamida o'lchanadi. Harorat … (°C) da … yordamida o'lchanadi. Aniq o'lchash … natija olishga yordam beradi. Tajribada har bir vazifa uchun mos … foydalanishim kerak.</p>`];
})(PAGES_TR.g7);
(function (P) {
P[6] = [`<h2>Toza moddalar, aralashmalar va eritmalar</h2>
<p>Tabiatdagi moddalar turli tarkibga ega. Ular toza moddalar va aralashmalarga bo'linadi. Aralashmalar gomogen (bir fazali) yoki geterogen (ikki va undan ortiq fazali) bo'lishi mumkin. Eritmalar — gomogen aralashmaning bir turi.</p>
<p><b>Asosiy savol:</b> Moddalar qanday tasniflanadi va ular o'rtasidagi farq nimada?</p>
<h3>Moddalarning tasnifi (tarkibiga ko'ra)</h3>
<p><b>1. Toza moddalar</b> — tarkibi va xossalari doimiy bo'lgan modda. Misollar: osh tuzi (NaCl), mis (Cu), kislorod (O₂), distillangan suv (H₂O).</p>
<p><b>2. Aralashmalar</b> — tarkibi o'zgaruvchan bo'lgan ikki yoki undan ortiq modda birikmasi.</p>
<ul><li><b>2.1. Gomogen aralashma (eritma)</b> — butun hajmi bo'ylab bir xil tarkib (bir faza). Misollar: tuz eritmasi (tuz + suv), shakar eritmasi (shakar + suv).</li><li><b>2.2. Geterogen aralashma</b> — notekis tarkib (bir nechta faza). Misollar: qum + suv, salat.</li></ul>
<h3>Taqqoslash jadvali</h3>
<table><tr><th>Belgi</th><th>Toza modda</th><th>Gomogen aralashma</th><th>Geterogen aralashma</th></tr><tr><td>Tarkibi</td><td>Doimiy</td><td>O'zgaruvchan, lekin bir xil</td><td>O'zgaruvchan, notekis</td></tr><tr><td>Ko'rinishi</td><td>Bir turdagi</td><td>Tiniq yoki rangli, lekin bir faza</td><td>Qismlari oddiy ko'z bilan ko'rinadi</td></tr><tr><td>Tarkibiy qismlari ko'rinadimi?</td><td>Yo'q</td><td>Yo'q</td><td>Ha</td></tr><tr><td>Fizik usullar bilan ajratish mumkinmi?</td><td>Yo'q (kimyoviy usullar kerak)</td><td>Ha (bug'latish, haydash, kristallash va h.k.)</td><td>Ha (filtrlash, magnit bilan ajratish, dekantatsiya va h.k.)</td></tr><tr><td>Misollar</td><td>Mis, NaCl, O₂, distillangan suv</td><td>Sho'r suv, shakar eritmasi, havo</td><td>Qum + suv, yog' + suv, salat</td></tr></table>
<h3>Hayotiy misollar</h3><p>Quyidagilarni toza modda, gomogen yoki geterogen aralashma deb tasniflang: distillangan suv, sut, oltin (Au), temir (Fe), sho'r suv, havo, granit (tog' jinsi), yog'.</p>
<h3>O'ylang va yozing</h3><ol><li>Nima uchun sho'r suvdan tuzni filtrlash orqali ajratib bo'lmaydi?</li><li>Nima uchun havo gomogen aralashma hisoblanadi?</li><li>Kundalik hayotingizdan aralashmalarga ikkita misol keltiring va ular nima uchun gomogen yoki geterogen ekanini tushuntiring.</li></ol>
<h3>Uyga vazifa</h3><p>1. Quyidagi moddalarni tasniflang: shakar, havo, sut. 2. Har biriga bittadan misol yozing: a) toza modda; b) gomogen aralashma; c) geterogen aralashma.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Eslab qoling!</b> Toza modda — tarkibi va xossalari doimiy. Aralashma — ikki yoki undan ortiq modda birikmasi. Eritma — tarkibi bir xil bo'lgan gomogen aralashma (bir faza).</div>`,
`<h3>1. To'g'ri yoki noto'g'ri</h3><ol><li>Eritma aralashmaning bir turi.</li><li>Havo gomogen aralashma.</li><li>Qum + suv gomogen aralashma.</li><li>Osh tuzi (NaCl) toza modda.</li><li>Aralashmaning tarkibi o'zgarishi mumkin.</li><li>Distillangan suv toza modda.</li></ol>
<h3>2. Vaziyatni tahlil qiling</h3><p>1. Suv yog' bilan aralashmaydi, ikki alohida qatlam kuzatiladi. Bu qanday aralashma? 2. Shakar suvda eriydi va tiniq eritma hosil qiladi. Bu qanday aralashma? 3. Temir qipiqlari va qumni magnit yordamida ajratish mumkin. Bu qanday aralashma?</p>
<h3>3. Rasmlarga qarab modda turini yozing</h3><p>T — toza modda; G — gomogen aralashma (eritma); Ge — geterogen aralashma.</p>
<h3>4. To'g'ri juftlikni toping</h3><p>1. Toza modda; 2. Gomogen aralashma (eritma); 3. Geterogen aralashma; 4. Gomogen aralashma — A. Sho'r suv B. Distillangan suv C. Qum + suv …</p>
<h3>5. Jadvalni to'ldiring</h3><p>Berilgan moddalar uchun turini belgilang (toza modda / gomogen aralashma / geterogen aralashma): distillangan suv, osh tuzi (NaCl), shakar eritmasi, qum + suv, salat.</p>
<h3>6. O'ylang va yozing</h3><p>Eritma va geterogen aralashma o'rtasidagi asosiy farq nimada? Misollar keltiring.</p>
<h3>Uyga vazifa</h3><ol><li>Kundalik hayotdan har bir tur uchun 3 tadan misol keltiring: toza modda, gomogen aralashma (eritma), geterogen aralashma.</li><li>Tuz eritmasini tayyorlash uchun qanday moddalar va jihozlardan foydalanasiz? Bosqichlarni yozing.</li></ol>
<h3>Bugun nimani o'rgandim?</h3><p>Toza modda — … tarkib va xossalarga ega modda. Aralashma — … tarkibli ikki yoki undan ortiq modda birikmasi. Eritma — … tarkibli aralashma turi. Havo — … aralashma, chunki u bir nechta … dan iborat. Qum + suv — … aralashma, chunki uning tarkibiy qismlarini … usullar bilan ajratish mumkin.</p>`];

P[7] = [`<h2>Aralashmalarni ajratish usullari</h2>
<p>Aralashmadagi moddalarni fizik usullar yordamida ajratish mumkin. Usulni tanlash tarkibiy qismlar xossalaridagi farqlarga bog'liq (qattiq–suyuq, suyuq–suyuq, qattiq–qattiq va h.k.). Bu usullar aralashmalardan foydali moddalar olishga yordam beradi.</p>
<p><b>Asosiy savol:</b> Har bir turdagi aralashma uchun qaysi ajratish usuli eng mos va nima uchun?</p>
<h3>Aralashmalarni ajratishning asosiy usullari (6 ta usul)</h3>
<table><tr><th>Usul</th><th>Nima uchun ishlatiladi</th><th>Misol</th></tr><tr><td>1. Filtrlash</td><td>Erimaydigan qattiq moddani suyuqlikdan ajratish (filtr qog'ozi, voronka, filtrat).</td><td>Qum + suv aralashmasi</td></tr><tr><td>2. Dekantatsiya (quyib olish)</td><td>Idish tubiga cho'kkan qattiq moddani ajratish yoki aralashmaydigan suyuqliklarni ajratish.</td><td>Loy + suv aralashmasi (cho'kkan qattiq modda)</td></tr><tr><td>3. Bug'latish</td><td>Erituvchini (suyuqlikni) bug'latib, erigan qattiq moddani olish.</td><td>Sho'r suvdan osh tuzi olish</td></tr><tr><td>4. Haydash (distillyatsiya)</td><td>Suyuqlikni qaynatib va bug'ini kondensatlab ajratish va yig'ish.</td><td>Sho'r suvdan toza suv olish</td></tr><tr><td>5. Magnit bilan ajratish</td><td>Aralashmadan magnitlanuvchi qattiq moddalarni ajratish.</td><td>Temir qipiqlarini qumdan ajratish</td></tr><tr><td>6. Ajratgich voronka</td><td>Ikki aralashmaydigan suyuqlikni ajratish.</td><td>Yog' + suv aralashmasi</td></tr></table>
<h3>Ajratish usulini qanday tanlash kerak?</h3><p>Aralashma turi qanday? → <b>Qattiq + suyuq (erimaydigan qattiq modda)</b>: qum + suv → filtrlash yoki dekantatsiya. <b>Qattiq + qattiq (magnitlanuvchi qismi bormi?)</b>: qum + temir qipiqlari → magnit bilan ajratish (aks holda boshqa usullar). <b>Suyuqlikda erigan qattiq modda</b>: suv + tuz → qattiq modda kerakmi? — bug'latish; suyuqlik kerakmi? — haydash. <b>Ikki aralashmaydigan suyuqlik</b>: yog' + suv → ajratgich voronka.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Muhim!</b> Ajratish usullari moddalarning fizik xossalariga asoslanadi. Moddalarning tarkibi o'zgarmaydi; faqat ularning fizik holati yoki joylashuvi o'zgaradi. Usulni aralashma turi va tarkibiy qismlar xossalaridagi farqqa qarab tanlang.</div>
<h3>O'ylang va yozing</h3><p>Har bir usul uchun kundalik hayotdan bittadan misol keltiring: filtrlash, dekantatsiya, bug'latish, haydash, magnit bilan ajratish, ajratgich voronka.</p>
<h3>Bugun nimani o'rgandim?</h3><p>Filtrlash … ajratish uchun ishlatiladi. Bug'latish eritmadan … olish uchun ishlatiladi. Haydash … suyuqlik olish uchun ishlatiladi. Ajratgich voronka ikki … suyuqlik uchun ishlatiladi. Ajratish usullari moddalarning … ni o'zgartirmaydi.</p>`,
`<h3>1. To'g'ri yoki noto'g'ri</h3><ol><li>Filtrlashda filtr qog'ozi voronkani ushlab turish uchun ishlatiladi.</li><li>Dekantatsiyada tiniq suyuqlik yuqoridan quyib olinadi.</li><li>Bug'latishda suyuqlik qaynaydi va erituvchi bug'ga aylanadi.</li><li>Haydashda bug' kondensatlanib, suyuqlik olinadi.</li><li>Magnit aralashmadan magnitlanuvchi moddalarni ajrata oladi.</li><li>Ajratgich voronkada ikki aralashmaydigan suyuqlik ajratiladi.</li></ol>
<h3>2. Rasmlarga qarab ajratish usulini yozing</h3>
<h3>3. Vaziyatni tahlil qiling</h3><p>Har bir aralashma uchun eng mos usulni tanlang: 1. Qum + suv. 2. Loyqa suv (tiniq suyuqlikni ajratish). 3. Tuz eritmasi (tuz olish). 4. Sho'r suvdan toza suv olish. 5. Temir qipiqlari + qum. 6. Yog' + suv (aralashmaydigan suyuqliklar).</p>
<h3>4. Aralashmani usul bilan moslang</h3><p>1. Qum + suv 2. Sho'r suv (tuz olish) 3. Sho'r suv (toza suv olish) 4. Temir qipiqlari + qum 5. Yog' + suv 6. Loyqa suv (quyib olish) — A. Filtrlash B. Dekantatsiya C. Bug'latish D. Haydash E. Magnit bilan ajratish F. Ajratgich voronka.</p>
<h3>5. Jadvalni to'ldiring</h3><p>Aralashma — ajratish usuli — asosiy jihoz — olingan natija: qum + suv; tuz eritmasi; sho'r suv (toza suv); temir qipiqlari + qum; yog' + suv; loyqa suv.</p>
<h3>6. O'ylang va yozing</h3><p>Nima uchun sho'r suvdan toza suv olish uchun haydash ishlatiladi? Tarkibiy qismlarning xossalari yordamida tushuntiring.</p>
<h3>7. Qiyin savol</h3><p>Qum + tuz + suv aralashmasi berilgan. Ajratish tartibini, ishlatiladigan usulni va olinadigan moddani ko'rsatuvchi jadvalni to'ldiring (bosqich — qaysi modda ajratiladi — usul — nima olinadi). Misol: 1-bosqich — …</p>
<h3>Bugun nimani o'rgandim?</h3><p>Filtrlashda … filtr qog'ozini ushlab turadi. Dekantatsiyada tiniq suyuqlik … dan quyib olinadi. Bug'latishda suyuqlik … ga aylanadi va qattiq modda qoladi. Haydashda bug' … va … ga aylanadi. Magnit aralashmadan … moddalarni ajratadi. Ajratgich voronka ikki … suyuqlikni ajratish uchun ishlatiladi. Fizik ajratish usullari moddalarning … ni o'zgartirmaydi.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Eslab qoling!</b> Ajratish usullari moddalarning fizik xossalariga asoslanadi. Har bir usul aralashmaning ma'lum turiga mos keladi. Fizik ajratish usullari moddalar tarkibini o'zgartirmaydi.</div>`];

P[8] = [`<h2>Fizik va kimyoviy o'zgarishlar</h2>
<p>Moddalar issiqlik, yorug'lik, bosim yoki boshqa omillar ta'sirida o'zgarishi mumkin. Ba'zi o'zgarishlarda yangi modda hosil bo'lmaydi (fizik o'zgarish). Boshqalarida yangi modda hosil bo'ladi (kimyoviy o'zgarish). Keling, ularni farqlashni o'rganamiz!</p>
<p><b>Asosiy savol:</b> O'zgarish fizik yoki kimyoviy ekanini qanday bilish mumkin? Qaysi belgilar farqni aniqlashga yordam beradi?</p>
<table><tr><th>FIZIK O'ZGARISH</th><th>KIMYOVIY O'ZGARISH</th></tr><tr><td>Faqat moddaning tashqi ko'rinishi, shakli yoki holati o'zgaradigan o'zgarish. Tarkib o'zgarmaydi. Yangi modda hosil bo'lmaydi.<br><b>Misollar:</b> muzning erishi, qog'ozning yirtilishi, shakarning suvda erishi, suvning qaynashi.<br>✓ Jarayon odatda qaytar.<br>✓ Moddaning tarkibi o'zgarmaydi.</td><td>Moddaning tarkibi o'zgarib, yangi modda (moddalar) hosil bo'ladigan o'zgarish. Yangi xossalar paydo bo'ladi.<br><b>Misollar:</b> temirning zanglashi, shamning yonishi, sirka + ichimlik sodasi (gaz hosil bo'ladi), tuxumning pishishi.<br>✓ Jarayon odatda qaytmas.<br>✓ Moddaning tarkibi o'zgaradi.</td></tr></table>
<h3>Kimyoviy o'zgarish belgilari</h3><p>Rangning o'zgarishi; gaz hosil bo'lishi; issiqlik va yorug'lik chiqishi yoki yutilishi; cho'kma (qattiq modda) hosil bo'lishi; yangi hid paydo bo'lishi; o'zgarish qaytmas.</p>
<h3>O'zgarishlarni tasniflang</h3><p>Fizik (F) yoki kimyoviy (K) ekanini aniqlang: 1. Muzning erishi 2. Suvning qaynashi 3. Shakarning suvda erishi 4. Temirning zanglashi 5. Yog'ochning yonishi 6. Qog'ozning yirtilishi 7. Tuxumning pishishi 8. Spirtning bug'lanishi.</p>
<h3>O'ylang va yozing</h3><p>Nima uchun quyidagilar fizik yoki kimyoviy o'zgarish ekanini tushuntiring: 1. Tuzning suvda erishi. 2. Temirning zanglashi. 3. Suvning qaynashi. 4. Shamning yonishi.</p>
<h3>Kundalik hayotdan misollar</h3><p>3 ta fizik va 3 ta kimyoviy o'zgarishga misol keltiring.</p>
<h3>Bugun nimani o'rgandim?</h3><p>Fizik o'zgarishda yangi … hosil bo'lmaydi. Kimyoviy o'zgarishda moddaning … o'zgaradi. Muzning erishi — … o'zgarish. Temirning zanglashi — … o'zgarish. Kimyoviy o'zgarish belgilariga … ning o'zgarishi, … hosil bo'lishi va … chiqishi yoki yutilishi kiradi.</p>`,
`<h3>1. To'g'ri yoki noto'g'ri</h3><ol><li>Muzning erishi kimyoviy o'zgarish.</li><li>Temirning zanglashi natijasida yangi modda hosil bo'ladi.</li><li>Tuzning suvda erishi fizik o'zgarish.</li><li>Yog'ochning yonishi kimyoviy o'zgarish.</li><li>Tuxumni pishirganda yangi modda hosil bo'ladi.</li><li>Suvning bug'lanishi fizik o'zgarish.</li></ol>
<h3>2. Rasmlarga qarab o'zgarish turini aniqlang</h3><p>Har bir rasm uchun fizik o'zgarish bo'lsa F, kimyoviy o'zgarish bo'lsa K yozing.</p>
<h3>3. Jadvalni to'ldiring</h3><p>Har bir misol uchun to'g'ri belgilarni belgilang (✓) — rang o'zgarishi / gaz hosil bo'lishi / issiqlik chiqishi yoki yutilishi / cho'kma hosil bo'lishi / yangi hid: sirka + ichimlik sodasi; temirning yonishi; tuxumni pishirish; misni (Cu) qizdirish; tuzni suvda eritish.</p>
<h3>4. Vaziyatni tahlil qiling</h3><p>Vaziyatlarni o'qing va fizik (F) yoki kimyoviy (K) o'zgarish deb aniqlang, sababini yozing: 1. Shakar suvda eriydi. 2. Temir ochiq havoda qolib zanglaydi. 3. Qog'oz mayda bo'laklarga yirtiladi. 4. Suv muzlatgichda muzlaydi.</p>
<h3>5. Tajriba: sirka + ichimlik sodasi reaksiyasi</h3><p>Rasmni kuzating va ko'rgan belgilaringizni belgilang (✓): rang o'zgarishi; gaz hosil bo'lishi; issiqlik chiqishi/yutilishi; yangi hid; cho'kma hosil bo'lishi. (Sirka — CH₃COOH, ichimlik sodasi — NaHCO₃.)</p>
<h3>6. O'ylang va yozing</h3><p>Nima uchun tuxumni pishirish fizik emas, kimyoviy o'zgarish hisoblanadi? Javobingizni misollar bilan asoslang.</p>
<h3>7. Taqqoslang va yozing</h3><p>Fizik va kimyoviy o'zgarishlarni taqqoslash jadvalini to'ldiring: yangi modda hosil bo'ladimi? tarkib o'zgaradimi? qaytarmi? umumiy belgilar (2–3); misollar (2–3).</p>
<h3>Bugun nimani o'rgandim?</h3><p>Fizik o'zgarishda yangi … hosil bo'lmaydi. Kimyoviy o'zgarishda moddaning … o'zgaradi va yangi … hosil bo'ladi. Muzning erishi — … o'zgarish, chunki kimyoviy tarkib o'zgarmaydi. Temirning zanglashi — … o'zgarish, chunki yangi modda hosil bo'ladi. Tuzning suvda erishi — … o'zgarish.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Eslab qoling!</b> Fizik o'zgarish — yangi modda hosil bo'lmaydi; faqat ko'rinish, shakl yoki holat o'zgaradi. Kimyoviy o'zgarish — yangi xossali yangi modda hosil bo'ladi. Kimyoviy o'zgarishlarda ko'pincha rang o'zgarishi, gaz hosil bo'lishi, issiqlik chiqishi/yutilishi, cho'kma hosil bo'lishi yoki yangi hid kuzatiladi.</div>`];

P[9] = [`<h2>Atom haqidagi tasavvurlarning rivojlanishi</h2>
<p>Atom haqidagi g'oyalar tajribalar, kuzatishlar va olimlarning fikrlari asosida rivojlangan. Har bir yangi model ba'zi xossalarni yaxshiroq tushuntirgan va oldingisining kamchiliklarini tuzatgan.</p>
<p><b>Asosiy savol:</b> Atom haqidagi ilmiy tasavvurlar vaqt o'tishi bilan qanday va nima uchun o'zgargan?</p>
<h3>Tarix davomida atom modellari</h3>
<table><tr><th>Demokrit</th><th>Dalton</th><th>Tomson</th><th>Rezerford</th><th>Bor</th><th>Zamonaviy model</th></tr><tr><td>Materiya atom deb ataluvchi juda kichik, bo'linmas zarrachalardan iborat. Atomlar ko'rinmas, buzilmas va doim harakatda.</td><td>Har bir element bir xil atomlardan iborat. Turli elementlarning atomlari massasi va o'lchami bilan farq qiladi. Atomlar qattiq sharlar.</td><td>Katod nurlari bilan tajribalarga asoslangan. Atomlar manfiy zaryadli elektronlarni o'z ichiga oladi. Atom — ichiga elektronlar joylashgan musbat zaryadli shar.</td><td>Oltin folga tajribasiga asoslangan. Atomning katta qismi bo'sh fazo. Markazda kichik, zich, musbat zaryadli yadro joylashgan. Elektronlar uning atrofida harakatlanadi.</td><td>Elektronlar yadro atrofida ma'lum energiya pog'onalarida (qobiqlarda) harakatlanadi. Elektronlar energiya yutib yoki chiqarib bir pog'onadan boshqasiga o'tishi mumkin.</td><td>Elektronlar aniq orbitalarda harakatlanmaydi. Ularning aniq joyini aniqlab bo'lmaydi. Elektronlar ehtimolligi yuqori sohalarda — elektron bulutida (orbitallarda) bo'ladi.</td></tr><tr><td>Qattiq, bo'linmas zarracha</td><td>Qattiq shar modeli</td><td>"Mayizli puding" modeli</td><td>Yadro modeli</td><td>Energiya pog'onalari modeli</td><td>Elektron buluti modeli (kvant modeli — Shryodinger va boshq.)</td></tr></table>
<h3>Fan qanday rivojlanadi?</h3><p>Yangi kuzatish va tajribalar paydo bo'ladi → eski model barcha kuzatishlarni tushuntira olmaydi → yangi model taklif etiladi yoki eskisi takomillashtiriladi → yangi tajriba va ma'lumotlar olinadi → fan rivojlanishda davom etadi.</p>
<h3>Jadvalni to'ldiring</h3><p>Olim — yil — modelning asosiy g'oyasi — sxema (oddiy): Demokrit, Dalton, Tomson, Rezerford, Bor, zamonaviy model.</p>
<h3>Vaziyatni tahlil qiling</h3><p>Fikr modelning xususiyati bo'lsa X, kamchiligi bo'lsa K yozing: 1. Dalton modelida atomlar bo'linmas. 2. Tomson modeli oltin folga tajribasi natijalarini tushuntira olmadi. 3. Rezerford atomning katta qismi bo'sh fazo ekanini ko'rsatdi. 4. Bor modeli elementlarning chiziqli spektrlari borligini tushuntirdi. 5. Zamonaviy model elektronlarni elektron buluti sifatida ko'rsatadi.</p>
<h3>O'ylang va yozing</h3><p>Nima uchun olimlar atom modelini o'zgartirishgan? 2–3 ta sabab keltiring.</p>
<h3>Bugun nimani o'rgandim?</h3><p>Ilmiy model … uchun yaratiladi. Demokrit materiya … dan iborat deb hisoblagan. Tomson atomlarda … borligini ko'rsatdi. Rezerford markazda kichik, zich, … ni kashf etdi. Bor elektronlar … da harakatlanadi deb taklif qildi. Zamonaviy modelda elektronlar … da bo'ladi.</p>
<div class="box y"><b>Eslab qoling!</b> Ilmiy g'oyalar tajribalar va yangi dalillar asosida vaqt o'tishi bilan rivojlanadi. Har bir yangi atom modeli ba'zi xossalarni yaxshiroq tushuntirgan va oldingi kamchiliklarni tuzatgan. Zamonaviy atom modeli elektronlarni qat'iy orbitalar emas, elektron buluti sifatida tasvirlaydi.</div>`,
`<h3>1. To'g'ri yoki noto'g'ri</h3><ol><li>Demokrit materiya juda kichik, bo'linmas zarrachalardan iborat degan modelni taklif qilgan.</li><li>Dalton bir elementning barcha atomlari bir xil deb da'vo qilgan.</li><li>Tomson katod nurlari tajribalari yordamida elektronni kashf etgan.</li><li>Rezerford musbat zaryad va massaning katta qismi kichik yadroda to'plangan ekanini ko'rsatgan.</li><li>Bor elektronlar yadro atrofida ma'lum energiya pog'onalarida harakatlanadi deb taklif qilgan.</li><li>Zamonaviy (kvant) model elektronlarni qat'iy orbitalar emas, elektron buluti sifatida tasvirlaydi.</li></ol>
<h3>2. Rasmlarni olimlar bilan moslang</h3><p>Har bir atom modelini to'g'ri olim bilan moslang: Dalton, Tomson, Rezerford, Bor, zamonaviy model (Shryodinger).</p>
<h3>3. Jadvalni to'ldiring</h3><p>Olim — davr — asosiy g'oya / asosiy xususiyat — model nomi.</p>
<h3>4. Sababini yozing</h3><ol><li>Nima uchun Dalton atomni "qattiq shar" deb tasvirlagan?</li><li>Nima uchun Dalton modeli katod nurlari tajribalarini tushuntirishga yetarli emas edi?</li><li>Nima uchun Tomson modeli oltin folga tajribasini tushuntirishga yetarli emas edi?</li><li>Nima uchun Rezerford modeli atom spektrlarini (chiziqli spektrlarni) tushuntirishga yetarli emas edi?</li></ol>
<h3>5. Test savollari</h3><ol><li>Qaysi olim katod nurlari tajribalari yordamida elektronni kashf etgan? A) Dalton B) Tomson C) Rezerford D) Bor</li><li>Qaysi model elektronlar ma'lum energiya pog'onalarida harakatlanadi deb hisoblaydi? A) Dalton B) Tomson C) Rezerford D) Bor</li><li>Qaysi modelda musbat zaryad va massaning katta qismi kichik yadroda to'plangan? A) Tomson B) Rezerford C) Bor D) Demokrit</li><li>Qaysi model elektronlarni elektron buluti sifatida tasvirlaydi (qat'iy orbitalarsiz)? A) Orbita modeli B) Mayizli puding modeli C) Qattiq shar modeli D) Kvant modeli</li></ol>
<h3>6. O'ylang va yozing</h3><p>Sizningcha, Rezerfordning yupqa oltin folga bilan o'tkazgan tajribasi nima uchun juda muhim bo'lgan? Zamonaviy atom modeliga ko'ra elektronlar qat'iy orbitalarda harakatlanmaydi. Elektron buluti modeli elektronlar xatti-harakatini qanday yaxshiroq tushuntiradi? Misollar keltiring.</p>
<h3>7. Atom modellarini taqqoslang</h3><p>Jadvalni to'ldiring: model — asosiy xususiyati — elektronlar qanday tasvirlangan — oldingi modeldan farqi.</p>
<h3>Bugun nimani o'rgandim?</h3><p>Atom nazariyasiga ko'ra materiya juda kichik … dan iborat. Bir elementning atomlari … , lekin turli elementlarning atomlari … . Elektronni … kashf etgan. Atom markazida … deb ataladigan kichik, zich, musbat zaryadli qism bor. Bor modelida elektronlar … da harakatlanadi. Zamonaviy modelda elektronlar … sifatida tasvirlanadi.</p>`];

P[10] = [`<h2>Zamonaviy atom modeli</h2>
<p>Atom — moddalarni tashkil etuvchi eng kichik zarracha. Atom tuzilishi olimlar tomonidan uzoq vaqt davomida turli usullar bilan o'rganilgan. Har bir yangi tajriba oldingi modelni takomillashtirgan yoki almashtirgan. Zamonaviy modelga ko'ra elektronlar yadro atrofida elektron bulutida joylashgan. Bu zamonaviy atom modeli deyiladi.</p>
<h3>1. Atom haqidagi g'oyalar qanday rivojlangan?</h3><ul><li><b>Dalton modeli</b> — atom juda kichik, bo'linmas qattiq zarracha.</li><li><b>Tomson modeli</b> — atomda elektronlar bor. Musbat zaryad butun atom bo'ylab tarqalgan.</li><li><b>Rezerford modeli</b> — atomda kichik, zich, musbat zaryadli yadro bor. Atomning katta qismi bo'sh fazo.</li><li><b>Bor modeli</b> — elektronlar yadro atrofida ma'lum energiya pog'onalarida (qobiqlarda) harakatlanadi.</li><li><b>Zamonaviy model</b> — elektronlar qat'iy orbitalarda harakatlanmaydi. Ular yadro atrofidagi elektron bulutida joylashgan.</li></ul>
<h3>2. Zamonaviy atom modeli</h3><p><b>Atomning asosiy qismi — yadro (p⁺ + n⁰).</b> Yadro protonlar va neytronlardan iborat. Yadro musbat zaryadga ega. Atom massasining deyarli hammasi yadroda to'plangan.</p><p><b>Elektron buluti.</b> Elektronlar elektronni topish ehtimolligi yuqori bo'lgan sohada harakatlanadi. Elektronning aniq joyini aniqlab bo'lmaydi. Zamonaviy modelda elektron buluti elektronning aniq harakat yo'lini emas, balki elektronlar ko'proq uchraydigan sohani ko'rsatadi.</p>
<h3>3. Bor modeli va zamonaviy model: asosiy farqlar</h3><table><tr><th>Bor modeli (1913)</th><th>Zamonaviy model (hozirgi)</th></tr><tr><td>• Elektronlar ma'lum energiya pog'onalarida (qobiqlarda) harakatlanadi.<br>• Elektronlar qat'iy aylana orbitalarda harakatlanadi.<br>• Soddalashtirilgan (ideallashtirilgan) model.</td><td>• Elektronlar qat'iy orbitalarda harakatlanmaydi.<br>• Elektronlar yadro atrofidagi elektron bulutida joylashgan.<br>• Energiya pog'onalari hali ham ishlatiladi, lekin elektronlar aniq orbitada emas.<br>• Atom tuzilishini aniqroq tasvirlaydi.</td></tr></table>
<h3>4. Atomdagi zarrachalar</h3><table><tr><th>Zarracha</th><th>Belgisi</th><th>Zaryadi</th><th>Joylashuvi</th><th>Asosiy roli</th></tr><tr><td>Proton</td><td>p⁺</td><td>+1</td><td>Yadroda</td><td>Tartib raqamini (element turini) belgilaydi.</td></tr><tr><td>Neytron</td><td>n⁰</td><td>0</td><td>Yadroda</td><td>Atom massasiga hissa qo'shadi.</td></tr><tr><td>Elektron</td><td>e⁻</td><td>−1</td><td>Elektron bulutida</td><td>Atomning kimyoviy xossalarini belgilaydi.</td></tr></table>
<div class="box y"><b>Eslab qoling!</b> Atom — moddalarni tashkil etuvchi juda kichik zarracha. Yadro protonlar va neytronlardan iborat. Zamonaviy modelda elektronlar elektron buluti sifatida ko'rsatiladi.</div>`,
`<h3>1. To'g'ri javobni tanlang</h3><ol><li>Qaysi model elektronlarni elektron bulutida deb tasvirlaydi? A) Ma'lum orbitalarda harakatlanadi B) Elektron bulutida tartibsiz harakatlanadi C) Faqat yadroda D) Atomdan tashqarida</li><li>Atomning qaysi qismida massaning katta qismi bor? A) Elektronlar B) Elektron buluti C) Yadro D) Atomdan tashqarida</li><li>Yadroning musbat zaryadini nima hosil qiladi? A) Neytronlar B) Elektronlar C) Protonlar D) Protonlar va elektronlar birga</li><li>Qaysi olim elektronlar ma'lum energiya pog'onalarida harakatlanadi deb taklif qilgan? A) Dalton B) Tomson C) Rezerford D) Bor</li><li>Qaysi modelda elektronlar elektron buluti sifatida tasvirlangan? A) Elektron buluti modeli B) Mayizli puding modeli C) Energiya pog'onalari modeli D) Yadro modeli</li><li>Quyidagilardan qaysi biri Bor modelining kamchiligi? A) Atom massasini tushuntira olmaydi B) Elektron bulutini ko'rsatmaydi C) Elektronlar harakatlanmaydi D) Yadro yo'q</li></ol>
<p><i>Zamonaviy modelda elektronning aniq joyini aniqlab bo'lmaydi. Biz faqat elektron ko'proq uchraydigan sohani ayta olamiz. Bu soha elektron buluti deyiladi.</i></p>
<h3>2. To'g'ri yoki noto'g'ri</h3><ol><li>Atomning asosiy qismi — yadro.</li><li>Elektronlar yadro ichida joylashgan.</li><li>Zamonaviy modelda elektronlar elektron bulutida tartibsiz harakatlanadi.</li><li>Neytronlar zaryadsiz zarrachalar.</li><li>Tomsonga ko'ra atomda musbat zaryadli qism bor.</li><li>Zamonaviy modelda elektron buluti elektronni topish ehtimolligi yuqori bo'lgan sohani ko'rsatadi.</li></ol>
<h3>3. O'ylab ko'ring!</h3><p>Agar elektronlar qat'iy orbitalarda harakatlanmasa, nima uchun biz ularning joylashuvini sxemalarda chizamiz? Bu sxemalar nimani ko'rsatadi? Sababi nima?</p>
<h3>4. Moslashtirish</h3><p>Proton — B) Musbat zaryadli zarracha; Neytron — A) Zaryadsiz zarracha; Elektron — C) Manfiy zaryadli zarracha; Yadro — D) Atomning asosiy qismi; Elektron buluti — E) Elektronni topish ehtimolligi yuqori soha.</p>
<h3>5. Savollarga javob bering</h3><ol><li>Nima uchun atom haqidagi tasavvur vaqt o'tishi bilan o'zgargan?</li><li>Bor modeli va zamonaviy model o'rtasidagi asosiy farq nimada?</li><li>Atom tuzilishini o'rganish kimyoda nimani tushunishga yordam beradi?</li><li>Nima uchun zamonaviy model elektronlarning aniq orbitalarini ko'rsata olmaydi?</li></ol>
<h3>6. Xatolarni toping va tuzating</h3><ol><li>Elektronlar yadro ichida joylashgan.</li><li>Neytronlar manfiy zaryadga ega.</li><li>Zamonaviy modelda elektronlar ma'lum orbitalarda harakatlanadi.</li><li>Atom bo'linmas zarracha va boshqa zarrachalarni o'z ichiga olmaydi.</li></ol>
<h3>7. Ijodiy vazifa — "Atom modellari vaqt chizig'i"</h3><p>Dalton, Tomson, Rezerford, Bor va zamonaviy modelni o'rganing. Jadvalni to'ldiring: model nomi — asosiy g'oya — oldingi modeldan farqi — nima uchun muhim. <b>Ilm kelajakni yorqinroq qiladi!</b></p>`];

P[11] = [`<h2>Atom yadrosi: proton va neytron</h2>
<p>Atom markazida juda kichik qism — yadro bor. Yadro protonlar va neytronlardan tashkil topgan. Protonlar musbat zaryadga ega, neytronlar esa zaryadsiz.</p>
<p><b>Asosiy savol:</b> Atom yadrosi nima? U qaysi zarrachalardan tashkil topgan?</p>
<h3>Asosiy g'oya</h3><p>Atom yadrosi butun atomga nisbatan juda kichik. U protonlar va neytronlardan iborat. Atom massasining deyarli hammasi yadroda to'plangan.</p>
<h3>Yadro nima va unda qaysi zarrachalar bor?</h3>
<table><tr><th>Proton (p⁺)</th><th>Neytron (n⁰)</th></tr><tr><td>Zaryadi: +1<br>Nisbiy massasi: ≈ 1 u<br>Joylashuvi: yadroda</td><td>Zaryadi: 0<br>Nisbiy massasi: ≈ 1 u<br>Joylashuvi: yadroda</td></tr></table>
<p><b>Yadro</b> — atom markazida joylashgan juda kichik qism. Atom diametri ≈ 10⁻¹⁰ m, yadro diametri ≈ 10⁻¹⁵ m. <b>Yadro atomdan taxminan 100 000 marta kichik!</b></p>
<div class="box">Barcha protonlar musbat zaryadga ega bo'lsa ham, yadro parchalanib ketmaydi. Uni kuchli yadro kuchlari ushlab turadi.</div>
<ul><li>✓ Yadro atom markazida joylashgan va juda kichik.</li><li>✓ Yadro protonlar va neytronlardan iborat.</li><li>✓ Proton (p⁺) musbat zaryadga ega (+1).</li><li>✓ Neytron (n⁰) zaryadga ega emas (0).</li><li>✓ Protonlar va neytronlar taxminan bir xil massaga ega (≈ 1 u).</li><li>✓ Atom massasining deyarli hammasi yadroda to'plangan.</li></ul>
<p><i>Keyingi darsda elektronlar va elektron qobiqlarni o'rganamiz.</i></p>`,
`<h3>1. To'g'ri yoki noto'g'ri</h3><ol><li>Yadro atom markazida joylashgan.</li><li>Yadroda faqat protonlar bor.</li><li>Neytron zaryadga ega emas.</li><li>Proton musbat zaryadga ega.</li><li>Atom massasining deyarli hammasi yadroda to'plangan.</li><li>Elektronlar yadro ichida joylashgan.</li></ol>
<h3>2. Jadvalni to'ldiring</h3><table><tr><th>Zarracha</th><th>Belgisi</th><th>Zaryadi</th><th>Joylashuvi</th><th>Nisbiy massasi</th></tr><tr><td>Proton</td><td>p⁺</td><td>+1</td><td>…</td><td>≈ 1 u</td></tr><tr><td>Neytron</td><td>n⁰</td><td>0</td><td>…</td><td>≈ 1 u</td></tr><tr><td>Elektron</td><td>…</td><td>…</td><td>…</td><td>1/1836</td></tr></table>
<h3>3. Test savollari</h3><ol><li>Protonlar va neytronlar qayerda joylashgan? A) Yadrodan tashqarida B) Atom markazida C) Elektron bulutida D) Elektron qobiqda</li><li>Protonning zaryadi qancha?</li><li>Neytronning zaryadi qancha?</li><li>Yadroni qaysi zarrachalar tashkil etadi? A) Faqat protonlar B) Faqat neytronlar C) Protonlar va neytronlar D) Elektronlar va protonlar</li></ol>
<h3>4. Rasmni tahlil qiling</h3><p>Yadro rasmiga qarang. Unda nechta proton va nechta neytron bor? Protonlar soni: … Neytronlar soni: … Zarrachalarning umumiy soni: …</p>
<h3>5. Zarrachalar nomini yozing</h3><p>Har bir rasm ostiga to'g'ri nomni yozing: proton (p⁺), neytron (n⁰), elektron (e⁻).</p>
<h3>6. Sababini yozing</h3><ol><li>Nima uchun atom massasining deyarli hammasi yadrodan keladi?</li><li>Protonlar bir xil zaryadga ega. Nima uchun ular yadroda bir-birini itarmaydi?</li><li>Yadroda neytronlarning vazifasi nima?</li></ol>
<h3>7. O'ylang va yozing</h3><p>Agar yadroda faqat bir nechta proton bo'lsa, atom massasining hammasi baribir yadroda bo'larmidi? Javobingizni tushuntiring.</p>
<h3>Bugun nimani o'rgandim?</h3><p>Atom markazida joylashgan juda kichik qism … deyiladi. Yadro … dan tashkil topgan. Protonning zaryadi … , belgisi … . Neytronning zaryadi … , belgisi … . Elektronlar yadro … joylashgan. Atom massasining deyarli hammasi … dan keladi.</p>`];
})(PAGES_TR.g7);
